window.LUMEN_LOAD({"id": "optimization", "lessons": {"problems": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"choosing-the-best-option\" data-h=\"3\">Choosing the best option</h3>\n<p>Every day you solve optimization problems without naming them. Which route gets you to work fastest? How many hours should you study each subject before an exam? How much should a café charge for a cup of lemonade? In each case there is something you control, something you want to make as small or as large as possible, and some rules you must respect. Machine learning is built on exactly the same idea: training a model means choosing its parameters to make a loss as small as possible.</p>\n<p>Imagine you run a lemonade stand. From experience, if you charge <span class=\"m\">p</span> euros per cup you sell <span class=\"m\">100 - 10p</span> cups a day, and each cup costs you 2 euros to make. Your daily profit is\n<span class=\"mb\">P(p) = (p - 2)(100 - 10p) = -10p^2 + 120p - 200</span>\nAt <span class=\"m\">p = 4</span> you make <span class=\"m\">2\\times60 = 120</span>; at <span class=\"m\">p = 8</span> you make <span class=\"m\">6\\times20 = 120</span> as well. Somewhere in between is a better price. The derivative <span class=\"m\">P'(p) = -20p + 120</span> is zero at <span class=\"m\">p = 6</span>, where the profit is <span class=\"m\">4\\times40 = 160</span>. Because <span class=\"m\">P'' = -20 &lt; 0</span> the curve is an upside-down bowl, so this is the best price. That small calculation contains every ingredient of an optimization problem.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-ingredients\" data-h=\"3\">The ingredients</h3>\n<p>The quantities you control are the <strong>decision variables</strong>, usually collected in a vector <span class=\"m\">\\theta</span> (in the example, the price <span class=\"m\">p</span>). The quantity you want to minimize or maximize is the <strong>objective</strong> <span class=\"m\">f(\\theta)</span>, also called the cost or loss. The rules are <strong>constraints</strong>, written as inequalities <span class=\"m\">g_i(\\theta)\\le0</span> or equalities <span class=\"m\">h_j(\\theta) = 0</span>. A price cannot be negative, for instance, so <span class=\"m\">-p\\le0</span>. The standard form is\n<span class=\"mb\">\\min_\\theta\\ f(\\theta)\\quad\\text{subject to}\\quad g_i(\\theta)\\le0,\\quad h_j(\\theta) = 0</span>\nThe set of all <span class=\"m\">\\theta</span> that satisfy the constraints is the <strong>feasible set</strong>. A problem with no constraints is called <strong>unconstrained</strong>. Maximizing <span class=\"m\">f</span> is the same as minimizing <span class=\"m\">-f</span>, so we only ever need to talk about minimization; the lemonade problem is <span class=\"m\">\\min_p\\ 10p^2 - 120p + 200</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Objective function</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The function <span class=\"m\">f(\\theta)</span> an optimization problem minimizes (or maximizes). In machine learning it is usually called the loss or cost.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Feasible set</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The set of points that satisfy all the constraints of an optimization problem. A point in it is called feasible.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"local-and-global-minima\" data-h=\"3\">Local and global minima</h3>\n<p>A point <span class=\"m\">\\theta^*</span> is a <strong>global minimum</strong> if <span class=\"m\">f(\\theta^*)\\le f(\\theta)</span> for every feasible <span class=\"m\">\\theta</span>. It is a <strong>local minimum</strong> if this holds only for <span class=\"m\">\\theta</span> near <span class=\"m\">\\theta^*</span>. Picture a hilly landscape: the global minimum is the lowest valley anywhere, while a local minimum is the bottom of any valley, even a small one high up a mountain.</p>\n<p>Here is a concrete function with both. Let <span class=\"m\">f(x) = \\frac{x^4}4 - \\frac{x^3}3 - x^2</span>. Its derivative is <span class=\"m\">f'(x) = x^3 - x^2 - 2x = x(x - 2)(x + 1)</span>, which is zero at <span class=\"m\">x = -1</span>, <span class=\"m\">0</span> and <span class=\"m\">2</span>. The values are <span class=\"m\">f(-1) = -\\frac5{12}\\approx-0.42</span>, <span class=\"m\">f(0) = 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">f(2) = -\\frac83\\approx-2.67</span>. So <span class=\"m\">x = 2</span> is the global minimum, <span class=\"m\">x = -1</span> is a local minimum that is not global, and <span class=\"m\">x = 0</span> is a local maximum. An algorithm that only walks downhill and starts at <span class=\"m\">x = -2</span> will settle at <span class=\"m\">x = -1</span> and never discover the better valley.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Local and global minimum</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\theta^*</span> is a global minimum if <span class=\"m\">f(\\theta^*)\\le f(\\theta)</span> for all feasible <span class=\"m\">\\theta</span>, and a local minimum if this holds for all feasible <span class=\"m\">\\theta</span> within some small distance of <span class=\"m\">\\theta^*</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"how-to-recognise-a-minimum\" data-h=\"3\">How to recognise a minimum</h3>\n<p>At an interior minimum of a smooth function the ground is flat: the derivative, or in many dimensions the gradient, is zero. This is the <strong>first-order condition</strong> <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f(\\theta^*) = \\mathbf 0</span>. Points where it holds are <strong>stationary points</strong>. But flat ground can also be a peak or a saddle, like the middle of a horse's saddle or a mountain pass, which goes up in one direction and down in another. The <strong>second-order condition</strong> settles it: if the second derivative is positive (in many dimensions, if the Hessian matrix is positive definite), the point is a strict local minimum. In the example, <span class=\"m\">f''(x) = 3x^2 - 2x - 2</span> is <span class=\"m\">3</span> at <span class=\"m\">x = -1</span>, <span class=\"m\">-2</span> at <span class=\"m\">x = 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">6</span> at <span class=\"m\">x = 2</span>, confirming two minima and one maximum.</p>\n<h3 id=\"does-a-minimum-even-exist\" data-h=\"3\">Does a minimum even exist?</h3>\n<p>Not always. The function <span class=\"m\">f(x) = 1/x</span> on <span class=\"m\">x &gt; 0</span> gets closer and closer to 0 but never reaches it, so it has no minimum. A linear function on the whole real line is unbounded below. The <strong>extreme value theorem</strong> gives a simple guarantee: a continuous function on a closed and bounded set always attains its minimum. In practice, losses that grow as parameters grow, such as a squared error plus a penalty on large weights, also have minima.</p>\n<h3 id=\"machine-learning-as-optimization\" data-h=\"3\">Machine learning as optimization</h3>\n<p>Training a model with parameters <span class=\"m\">\\theta</span> on data <span class=\"m\">(x_i, y_i)</span> almost always means solving\n<span class=\"mb\">\\min_\\theta\\ \\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n\\ell(f_\\theta(x_i), y_i) + \\lambda R(\\theta)</span>\nThe first term is the average loss on the training data, the <strong>empirical risk</strong>. The second is a <strong>regularizer</strong> that discourages overly complex models. Even very simple statistics are solutions of such problems. For the data <span class=\"m\">\\{1, 2, 6\\}</span>, the constant <span class=\"m\">c</span> that minimizes <span class=\"m\">\\sum_i(x_i - c)^2</span> is the mean, <span class=\"m\">3</span>, while the <span class=\"m\">c</span> that minimizes <span class=\"m\">\\sum_i|x_i - c|</span> is the median, <span class=\"m\">2</span>. The choice of loss decides which answer you get.</p>\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Assuming any point with zero gradient is a minimum: it may be a maximum or a saddle. Forgetting the boundary of the feasible set, where a constrained minimum often sits even though the gradient there is not zero. Assuming a minimum exists when the objective can keep decreasing forever. Mixing up \"minimize <span class=\"m\">f</span>\" with \"find where <span class=\"m\">f = 0</span>\".</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>An optimization problem has decision variables, an objective and constraints. A global minimum is the lowest feasible value; a local minimum is lowest only nearby. At an interior minimum the gradient vanishes, and positive curvature confirms it. Minima exist on closed bounded sets for continuous functions. Training a machine learning model is minimizing average loss plus a regularizer.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"gd\" data-args='{&quot;surface&quot;: &quot;bumpy&quot;, &quot;lr&quot;: 0.1}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> A bumpy loss surface with many valleys. Drag the start point, press Run, and see which valley gradient descent finds.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Press Run from the default start and note the final loss value.\n2. Drag the start point to a different valley and run again. Compare the final losses.\n3. Start very close to the centre <span class=\"m\">(0, 0)</span> and run. This is the global minimum, where the loss is 0.\n4. Switch the surface to the saddle <span class=\"m\">x^2 - y^2</span> and start on the <span class=\"m\">x</span>-axis. The path reaches the flat point <span class=\"m\">(0, 0)</span>, which is not a minimum.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> downhill methods find the bottom of whichever valley they start in, which is exactly the difference between local and global minima from the article. The saddle shows a stationary point (zero gradient) that is not a minimum; a tiny nudge off the axis makes the path escape downward.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\min_\\theta f(\\theta)\\ \\text{s.t.}\\ g_i(\\theta)\\le0,\\ h_j(\\theta) = 0\\qquad\\max f = -\\min(-f)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{first order: }\\nabla f(\\theta^*) = \\mathbf 0\\qquad\\text{second order: }\\nabla^2f(\\theta^*)\\succ0\\Rightarrow\\text{strict local min}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{empirical risk: }\\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n\\ell(f_\\theta(x_i), y_i) + \\lambda R(\\theta)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\arg\\min_c\\sum_i(x_i - c)^2 = \\bar x\\qquad\\arg\\min_c\\sum_i|x_i - c| = \\operatorname{median}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">First-order necessary condition</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">f</span> is differentiable and <span class=\"m\">\\theta^*</span> is a local minimum in the interior of the feasible set, then <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f(\\theta^*) = \\mathbf 0</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-title\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Suppose <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf g = \\nabla f(\\theta^*)\\ne\\mathbf 0</span>. For small <span class=\"m\">t &gt; 0</span>, <span class=\"m\">f(\\theta^* - t\\mathbf g) = f(\\theta^*) - t\\|\\mathbf g\\|^2 + o(t)</span>, which is smaller than <span class=\"m\">f(\\theta^*)</span> once <span class=\"m\">t</span> is small enough. That contradicts <span class=\"m\">\\theta^*</span> being a local minimum.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Extreme value theorem</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A continuous function on a closed and bounded (compact) set attains a minimum and a maximum.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Find and classify the stationary points of <span class=\"m\">f(x) = x^3 - 3x</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">f'(x) = 3x^2 - 3 = 0</span> gives <span class=\"m\">x = \\pm1</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">f''(x) = 6x</span>: <span class=\"m\">f''(1) = 6 &gt; 0</span>, a local minimum with <span class=\"m\">f(1) = -2</span>; <span class=\"m\">f''(-1) = -6 &lt; 0</span>, a local maximum with <span class=\"m\">f(-1) = 2</span>.</li>\n<li>Neither is global: <span class=\"m\">f(x)\\to-\\infty</span> as <span class=\"m\">x\\to-\\infty</span>, so there is no global minimum on the whole line.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Show that the mean minimizes the sum of squared deviations from <span class=\"m\">\\{1, 2, 6\\}</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">S(c) = (1 - c)^2 + (2 - c)^2 + (6 - c)^2</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">S'(c) = -2[(1 - c) + (2 - c) + (6 - c)] = -2(9 - 3c)</span>, zero at <span class=\"m\">c = 3</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">S''(c) = 6 &gt; 0</span>, so <span class=\"m\">c = 3</span> is the minimum, with <span class=\"m\">S(3) = 4 + 1 + 9 = 14</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A farmer has 40 metres of fence to enclose a rectangular pen against a long wall (the wall forms one side). (a) Write the problem in standard form with variables <span class=\"m\">x</span> (the two sides perpendicular to the wall) and <span class=\"m\">y</span> (the side parallel to it). (b) Solve it. (c) Explain why a maximum must exist.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) Maximize <span class=\"m\">xy</span> subject to <span class=\"m\">2x + y = 40</span>, <span class=\"m\">x\\ge0</span>, <span class=\"m\">y\\ge0</span>; equivalently minimize <span class=\"m\">-xy</span>.\n(b) Substitute <span class=\"m\">y = 40 - 2x</span>: area <span class=\"m\">A(x) = 40x - 2x^2</span>. <span class=\"m\">A'(x) = 40 - 4x = 0</span> at <span class=\"m\">x = 10</span>, so <span class=\"m\">y = 20</span> and the area is 200 square metres. <span class=\"m\">A'' = -4 &lt; 0</span> confirms a maximum.\n(c) The feasible values <span class=\"m\">0\\le x\\le20</span> form a closed bounded interval and <span class=\"m\">A</span> is continuous, so the extreme value theorem guarantees a maximum. The endpoints give area 0, so the maximum is at the interior stationary point.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Linear regression minimizes squared error, logistic regression minimizes cross-entropy, and neural networks minimize the same losses over millions of parameters. Regularization terms like <span class=\"m\">\\lambda\\|\\theta\\|^2</span> are part of the objective, and hyperparameter search is itself an outer optimization problem.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Many algorithm problems are optimization problems over discrete choices: shortest paths, minimum spanning trees, scheduling and the travelling salesman problem. Continuous optimization ideas such as relaxation and duality are used to design and analyse algorithms for them.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Airlines decide how many seats to sell at each fare (variables) to maximize revenue (objective) without exceeding the plane's capacity (constraint). This revenue management problem is solved by optimization software every day for millions of flights.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Maximizing <span class=\"m\">f</span> is equivalent to minimizing...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">-f</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">1/f</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">f^2</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Negating flips the order of values, so the largest <span class=\"m\">f</span> is the smallest <span class=\"m\">-f</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For the profit <span class=\"m\">P(p) = -10p^2 + 120p - 200</span>, what price maximizes profit?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"6\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">P'(p) = -20p + 120 = 0</span> gives <span class=\"m\">p = 6</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A stationary point with <span class=\"m\">f''(x^*) &lt; 0</span> is a...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">local minimum</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">local maximum</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">global minimum</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Negative curvature means the graph bends downward, a peak.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which value of <span class=\"m\">c</span> minimizes <span class=\"m\">\\sum_i|x_i - c|</span> for the data <span class=\"m\">\\{1, 2, 10\\}</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The sum of absolute deviations is minimized at the median.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Does <span class=\"m\">f(x) = e^{-x}</span> have a minimum on the whole real line?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Yes, at <span class=\"m\">x = 0</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">No, it approaches 0 but never reaches it</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Yes, at every point</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The infimum 0 is not attained, so there is no minimizer.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">At <span class=\"m\">(0, 0)</span> the function <span class=\"m\">x^2 - y^2</span> has zero gradient. This point is a...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">saddle point</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">local minimum</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">global maximum</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>It curves up along <span class=\"m\">x</span> and down along <span class=\"m\">y</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "convexity": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"bowls-versus-egg-cartons\" data-h=\"3\">Bowls versus egg cartons</h3>\n<p>Drop a marble into a smooth bowl and it rolls to the same bottom no matter where you release it. Drop it into an egg carton and it ends up in whichever cup is nearest. Optimization problems come in the same two flavours. The bowl-like ones are called <strong>convex</strong>, and they are wonderful: every downhill method reaches the true minimum. The egg-carton ones are <strong>non-convex</strong>, and you can get stuck. Knowing which kind of problem you have tells you how much you can trust the answer.</p>\n<h3 id=\"convex-sets\" data-h=\"3\">Convex sets</h3>\n<p>A set <span class=\"m\">C</span> is convex if, whenever two points <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf a</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf b</span> are in it, the whole straight segment between them is too. In symbols, <span class=\"m\">\\lambda\\mathbf a + (1 - \\lambda)\\mathbf b\\in C</span> for all <span class=\"m\">\\lambda</span> between 0 and 1. A disc, a square, a half-plane and a line are convex. A crescent moon or a doughnut is not, because some segments leave the shape. A very useful fact: the intersection of convex sets is convex, so a feasible set described by many linear inequalities is convex.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Convex set</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A set <span class=\"m\">C</span> such that for all <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf a, \\mathbf b\\in C</span> and all <span class=\"m\">\\lambda\\in[0, 1]</span>, the point <span class=\"m\">\\lambda\\mathbf a + (1 - \\lambda)\\mathbf b</span> is also in <span class=\"m\">C</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"convex-functions\" data-h=\"3\">Convex functions</h3>\n<p>A function is convex if the chord joining any two points on its graph lies on or above the graph:\n<span class=\"mb\">f(\\lambda x + (1 - \\lambda)y)\\le\\lambda f(x) + (1 - \\lambda)f(y)\\quad\\text{for all }\\lambda\\in[0, 1]</span>\nCheck it for <span class=\"m\">f(x) = x^2</span> with <span class=\"m\">x = 0</span>, <span class=\"m\">y = 2</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = \\frac12</span>. The midpoint of the inputs is 1, where <span class=\"m\">f(1) = 1</span>. The midpoint of the chord is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{0 + 4}2 = 2</span>. Since <span class=\"m\">1\\le2</span>, the chord is above. For <span class=\"m\">f(x) = \\sin x</span> with <span class=\"m\">x = 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">y = \\pi</span>, the midpoint value is <span class=\"m\">\\sin\\frac\\pi2 = 1</span> while the chord midpoint is 0, so the chord is below and <span class=\"m\">\\sin</span> is not convex there. A function is <strong>concave</strong> if <span class=\"m\">-f</span> is convex: chords lie below.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Convex function</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A function <span class=\"m\">f</span> on a convex set such that <span class=\"m\">f(\\lambda x + (1 - \\lambda)y)\\le\\lambda f(x) + (1 - \\lambda)f(y)</span> for all <span class=\"m\">x, y</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\lambda\\in[0, 1]</span>. It is strictly convex if the inequality is strict whenever <span class=\"m\">x\\ne y</span> and <span class=\"m\">0 &lt; \\lambda &lt; 1</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"three-practical-tests\" data-h=\"3\">Three practical tests</h3>\n<p>Checking chords directly is tedious, so we use equivalent tests. For a differentiable function, convexity means the graph lies above every tangent line: <span class=\"m\">f(y)\\ge f(x) + f'(x)(y - x)</span>. For a twice-differentiable function of one variable, it means <span class=\"m\">f''(x)\\ge0</span> everywhere. For many variables, it means the Hessian matrix is <strong>positive semidefinite</strong> everywhere, that is, all its eigenvalues are <span class=\"m\">\\ge0</span>.</p>\n<p>These tests show that <span class=\"m\">x^2</span>, <span class=\"m\">e^x</span>, <span class=\"m\">-\\log x</span> (for <span class=\"m\">x &gt; 0</span>) and <span class=\"m\">|x|</span> are convex, and that <span class=\"m\">x^3</span> is not convex on the whole line, because <span class=\"m\">f''(x) = 6x</span> is negative for <span class=\"m\">x &lt; 0</span>. Note that <span class=\"m\">x^4</span> is convex even though <span class=\"m\">f''(0) = 0</span>: the condition is <span class=\"m\">\\ge0</span>, not <span class=\"m\">&gt;0</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"building-convex-functions\" data-h=\"3\">Building convex functions</h3>\n<p>You rarely test complicated losses from scratch. Instead you use rules that preserve convexity. A sum of convex functions with nonnegative weights is convex. A convex function composed with a linear (affine) map, <span class=\"m\">f(A\\mathbf x + \\mathbf b)</span>, is convex. The pointwise maximum of convex functions is convex. Every norm is convex. With these rules, the least-squares loss <span class=\"m\">\\|X\\mathbf w - \\mathbf y\\|^2</span>, the logistic regression loss, the SVM hinge loss <span class=\"m\">\\max(0, 1 - y\\,\\mathbf w^T\\mathbf x)</span> and the lasso penalty <span class=\"m\">\\lambda\\|\\mathbf w\\|_1</span> are all convex. Be careful: a product or a difference of convex functions need not be convex, and <span class=\"m\">-\\|\\mathbf x\\|^2</span> is concave.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-convexity-is-so-valuable\" data-h=\"3\">Why convexity is so valuable</h3>\n<p>For a convex function, <strong>every local minimum is a global minimum</strong>, and for a differentiable convex function, any point with <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f = \\mathbf 0</span> is a global minimum. There are no false valleys to get stuck in. If the function is strictly convex, the minimizer is unique. This is why convex problems are considered \"solved\": efficient algorithms reliably find the optimum. Neural network losses are not convex, yet gradient methods work well on them in practice, a fact that is still an active research area.</p>\n<h3 id=\"jensen-s-inequality\" data-h=\"3\">Jensen's inequality</h3>\n<p>Convexity also gives one of the most used inequalities in probability. If <span class=\"m\">f</span> is convex and <span class=\"m\">X</span> is a random variable,\n<span class=\"mb\">f(\\mathbb E[X])\\le\\mathbb E[f(X)]</span>\nSuppose <span class=\"m\">X</span> is 0 or 2 with equal probability and <span class=\"m\">f(x) = x^2</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">f(\\mathbb E[X]) = f(1) = 1</span> while <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb E[f(X)] = \\frac{0 + 4}2 = 2</span>. The gap, <span class=\"m\">2 - 1 = 1</span>, is exactly the variance of <span class=\"m\">X</span>, which is another way to see that a variance is never negative. The chord definition is Jensen's inequality for a variable with two values.</p>\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Confusing convex (curves up, chords above) with concave (curves down). Requiring <span class=\"m\">f'' &gt; 0</span> when <span class=\"m\">f''\\ge0</span> is enough. Assuming the product or difference of convex functions is convex. Checking only the diagonal of the Hessian: <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 3\\\\3 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span> has positive diagonal entries but an eigenvalue of <span class=\"m\">-2</span>, so it is not positive semidefinite.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Convex sets contain the segment between any two of their points; convex functions have chords above their graphs. Test with tangents, <span class=\"m\">f''\\ge0</span>, or a positive semidefinite Hessian, and build complicated convex functions from simple ones with sums, affine maps and maxima. For convex functions every local minimum is global. Jensen's inequality says <span class=\"m\">f(\\mathbb E[X])\\le\\mathbb E[f(X)]</span>.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"convex\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Drag the two chord endpoints. For a convex function the chord never dips below the curve.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Choose <span class=\"m\">x^2</span> and drag the endpoints anywhere. The chord always stays above the curve.\n2. Choose <span class=\"m\">|x|</span> and put one endpoint on each side of 0. The chord is above even at the kink, so convexity does not need a derivative.\n3. Choose <span class=\"m\">x^4 - 3x^2</span> and place both endpoints near <span class=\"m\">x = \\pm1</span>. Find a chord that dips below the curve.\n4. Choose <span class=\"m\">\\sin x</span> and find a position where the chord is above and another where it is below.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the chord test from the article is a test you can see. Wherever you can make a chord go below the graph, the function has a bump, and a bump is exactly what creates a second valley where gradient descent can get stuck.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">f(\\lambda x + (1 - \\lambda)y)\\le\\lambda f(x) + (1 - \\lambda)f(y),\\quad\\lambda\\in[0, 1]</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">f(y)\\ge f(x) + \\nabla f(x)^T(y - x)\\qquad f''\\ge0\\qquad\\nabla^2f\\succeq0</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">f(\\mathbb E[X])\\le\\mathbb E[f(X)]\\qquad(f\\text{ convex})</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{convex: }x^2,\\ e^x,\\ -\\log x,\\ |x|,\\ \\|\\mathbf x\\|,\\ \\log\\textstyle\\sum_ie^{x_i},\\ \\max(0, 1 - t)</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Local minima of convex functions are global</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">f</span> is convex and <span class=\"m\">x^*</span> is a local minimum, then <span class=\"m\">f(x^*)\\le f(y)</span> for every <span class=\"m\">y</span> in the domain.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-title\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Suppose <span class=\"m\">f(y) &lt; f(x^*)</span> for some <span class=\"m\">y</span>. For small <span class=\"m\">\\lambda &gt; 0</span>, the point <span class=\"m\">z = (1 - \\lambda)x^* + \\lambda y</span> is close to <span class=\"m\">x^*</span>, and convexity gives <span class=\"m\">f(z)\\le(1 - \\lambda)f(x^*) + \\lambda f(y) &lt; f(x^*)</span>. So there are points arbitrarily close to <span class=\"m\">x^*</span> with smaller value, contradicting that <span class=\"m\">x^*</span> is a local minimum.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Jensen's inequality</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">f</span> is convex and <span class=\"m\">X</span> is a random variable with finite mean, then <span class=\"m\">f(\\mathbb E[X])\\le\\mathbb E[f(X)]</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-title\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Let <span class=\"m\">\\mu = \\mathbb E[X]</span>. A convex function lies above a supporting line through <span class=\"m\">(\\mu, f(\\mu))</span>: <span class=\"m\">f(x)\\ge f(\\mu) + c(x - \\mu)</span> for some slope <span class=\"m\">c</span> (for differentiable <span class=\"m\">f</span>, <span class=\"m\">c = f'(\\mu)</span>). Take expectations of both sides: <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb E[f(X)]\\ge f(\\mu) + c(\\mathbb E[X] - \\mu) = f(\\mu)</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Show <span class=\"m\">f(x) = x^2 - 4x + 7</span> is convex and find its minimum</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">f''(x) = 2\\ge0</span> everywhere, so <span class=\"m\">f</span> is convex (in fact strictly convex).</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">f'(x) = 2x - 4 = 0</span> at <span class=\"m\">x = 2</span>.</li>\n<li>By convexity this stationary point is the global minimum: <span class=\"m\">f(2) = 4 - 8 + 7 = 3</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Is <span class=\"m\">f(x, y) = x^2 + xy + y^2</span> convex?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Hessian: <span class=\"m\">H = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span>, constant everywhere.</li>\n<li>Eigenvalues: <span class=\"m\">\\det(H - tI) = (2 - t)^2 - 1 = 0</span> gives <span class=\"m\">t = 1</span> and <span class=\"m\">t = 3</span>.</li>\n<li>Both are positive, so <span class=\"m\">H</span> is positive definite and <span class=\"m\">f</span> is strictly convex, with unique minimum at <span class=\"m\">(0, 0)</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Use Jensen to prove the AM-GM inequality for two numbers</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\log</span> is concave, so Jensen reverses: <span class=\"m\">\\log\\mathbb E[X]\\ge\\mathbb E[\\log X]</span>.</li>\n<li>With <span class=\"m\">X</span> equal to <span class=\"m\">a</span> or <span class=\"m\">b</span> with probability <span class=\"m\">\\frac12</span> each: <span class=\"m\">\\log\\frac{a + b}2\\ge\\frac{\\log a + \\log b}2 = \\log\\sqrt{ab}</span>.</li>\n<li>Exponentiate: <span class=\"m\">\\frac{a + b}2\\ge\\sqrt{ab}</span>. For <span class=\"m\">a = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">b = 9</span>: <span class=\"m\">5\\ge3</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Let <span class=\"m\">f(x, y) = x^2 + 3xy + y^2</span>. (a) Compute its Hessian and eigenvalues. (b) Is <span class=\"m\">f</span> convex? (c) Find two points whose chord midpoint lies below the graph, confirming your answer.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) <span class=\"m\">H = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 3\\\\3 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span>, with <span class=\"m\">(2 - t)^2 = 9</span>, so <span class=\"m\">t = 5</span> or <span class=\"m\">t = -1</span>.\n(b) No. An eigenvalue is negative, so <span class=\"m\">H</span> is not positive semidefinite.\n(c) Along the eigenvector direction <span class=\"m\">(1, -1)</span>, <span class=\"m\">f(t, -t) = t^2 - 3t^2 + t^2 = -t^2</span>, which is concave. Take <span class=\"m\">(-1, 1)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(1, -1)</span>, where <span class=\"m\">f = -1</span>. The midpoint <span class=\"m\">(0, 0)</span> has <span class=\"m\">f = 0</span>, but the chord midpoint is <span class=\"m\">-1</span>. The chord lies below the graph.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Linear regression, logistic regression, SVMs and the lasso are convex, so their training finds the global optimum and the answer does not depend on initialization. Jensen's inequality proves that KL divergence is nonnegative and gives the evidence lower bound (ELBO) used to train variational autoencoders.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Convex problems can be solved to high accuracy in polynomial time by interior-point methods. Modelling tools such as CVXPY check convexity automatically using exactly the composition rules in this lesson, a system called disciplined convex programming.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Investment managers use Markowitz portfolio optimization: choose portfolio weights to minimize risk (a convex quadratic <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf w^T\\Sigma\\mathbf w</span>) subject to a target expected return and weights summing to 1. Because the problem is convex, the computed portfolio is guaranteed to be the best one under the model.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Is <span class=\"m\">f(x) = e^x</span> convex?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">Yes</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">No</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">f''(x) = e^x &gt; 0</span> everywhere.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Is <span class=\"m\">\\sin x</span> convex on the whole real line?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Yes</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">No</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">f''(x) = -\\sin x</span> is negative on <span class=\"m\">(0, \\pi)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Is <span class=\"m\">f(x, y) = x^2 - y^2</span> convex?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Yes</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">No</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Its Hessian has eigenvalues 2 and <span class=\"m\">-2</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The pointwise maximum of two convex functions is...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">always convex</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">never convex</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">convex only if both are linear</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>If both chords lie above each function, they lie above the maximum.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">X</span> is 1 or 3 with equal probability and <span class=\"m\">f(x) = x^2</span>. What is <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb E[f(X)] - f(\\mathbb E[X])</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\mathbb E[X^2] = 5</span> and <span class=\"m\">(\\mathbb E X)^2 = 4</span>; the gap is the variance.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">f(x) = x^4</span> has <span class=\"m\">f''(0) = 0</span>. Is it convex?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">Yes, because <span class=\"m\">f''(x) = 12x^2\\ge0</span> everywhere</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">No, because <span class=\"m\">f''(0)</span> is not positive</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Convexity needs <span class=\"m\">f''\\ge0</span>, not <span class=\"m\">&gt; 0</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "gradient-descent": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"walking-down-a-hill-in-fog\" data-h=\"3\">Walking down a hill in fog</h3>\n<p>Imagine you are on a hillside in thick fog and want to reach the bottom of the valley. You cannot see the valley, but you can feel which way the ground slopes under your feet. A sensible plan is to take a step in the steepest downhill direction, feel the slope again, and repeat. Gradient descent is exactly this plan. It needs no map of the whole landscape, only the local slope, which is why it works even for neural networks with billions of parameters.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-update-rule\" data-h=\"3\">The update rule</h3>\n<p>For a differentiable function <span class=\"m\">f(\\theta)</span>, the gradient <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f(\\theta)</span> points in the direction of steepest increase, so <span class=\"m\">-\\nabla f(\\theta)</span> points in the direction of steepest decrease. Gradient descent repeats\n<span class=\"mb\">\\theta_{t+1} = \\theta_t - \\eta\\,\\nabla f(\\theta_t)</span>\nwhere <span class=\"m\">\\eta &gt; 0</span> is the <strong>learning rate</strong> or step size. Why is <span class=\"m\">-\\nabla f</span> the steepest direction? The rate of change of <span class=\"m\">f</span> in a unit direction <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf u</span> is <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f^T\\mathbf u</span>. By the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality this is at least <span class=\"m\">-\\|\\nabla f\\|</span>, with equality exactly when <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf u</span> points along <span class=\"m\">-\\nabla f</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Gradient descent</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The iterative method <span class=\"m\">\\theta_{t+1} = \\theta_t - \\eta\\nabla f(\\theta_t)</span>, which repeatedly steps in the direction of steepest decrease of <span class=\"m\">f</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Learning rate</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The positive step size <span class=\"m\">\\eta</span> in gradient descent. Too small makes progress slow; too large makes the iterates oscillate or diverge.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"a-first-example\" data-h=\"3\">A first example</h3>\n<p>Take <span class=\"m\">f(x) = x^2</span>, so <span class=\"m\">f'(x) = 2x</span>, and start at <span class=\"m\">x_0 = 4</span> with <span class=\"m\">\\eta = 0.25</span>. The update is <span class=\"m\">x\\leftarrow x - 0.25\\cdot2x = 0.5x</span>, so the iterates are <span class=\"m\">4, 2, 1, 0.5, \\ldots</span>, halving each time and heading to the minimum at 0. With <span class=\"m\">\\eta = 0.1</span> the update is <span class=\"m\">x\\leftarrow0.8x</span>: steady but slower. With <span class=\"m\">\\eta = 1</span> it is <span class=\"m\">x\\leftarrow-x</span>, which bounces between <span class=\"m\">4</span> and <span class=\"m\">-4</span> forever. With <span class=\"m\">\\eta = 1.5</span> it is <span class=\"m\">x\\leftarrow-2x</span>, giving <span class=\"m\">4, -8, 16, \\ldots</span>: divergence. The learning rate decides everything.</p>\n<h3 id=\"exactly-when-does-it-converge\" data-h=\"3\">Exactly when does it converge?</h3>\n<p>We can analyse any one-dimensional quadratic <span class=\"m\">f(x) = \\frac a2x^2</span> with curvature <span class=\"m\">a &gt; 0</span>. Here <span class=\"m\">f'(x) = ax</span> and the update is <span class=\"m\">x\\leftarrow(1 - \\eta a)x</span>. Each step multiplies the distance to the minimum by <span class=\"m\">|1 - \\eta a|</span>. This shrinks the distance exactly when <span class=\"m\">|1 - \\eta a| &lt; 1</span>, that is,\n<span class=\"mb\">0 &lt; \\eta &lt; \\frac2a</span>\nThe best choice is <span class=\"m\">\\eta = 1/a</span>, which reaches the minimum in one step. Between <span class=\"m\">1/a</span> and <span class=\"m\">2/a</span> the iterates overshoot and alternate sides but still converge. Above <span class=\"m\">2/a</span> they diverge. For <span class=\"m\">f(x) = x^2</span>, <span class=\"m\">a = 2</span>, so the limit is <span class=\"m\">\\eta &lt; 1</span>, matching the example.</p>\n<h3 id=\"many-dimensions-and-the-condition-number\" data-h=\"3\">Many dimensions and the condition number</h3>\n<p>Now take the bowl <span class=\"m\">f(x, y) = x^2 + 2y^2</span>. Its gradient is <span class=\"m\">(2x, 4y)</span>, so each coordinate evolves independently: <span class=\"m\">x\\leftarrow(1 - 2\\eta)x</span> and <span class=\"m\">y\\leftarrow(1 - 4\\eta)y</span>. Both must shrink, so <span class=\"m\">\\eta &lt; 2/4 = 0.5</span>. The <strong>steepest</strong> direction sets the speed limit, and the learning rate must satisfy <span class=\"m\">\\eta &lt; 2/L</span>, where <span class=\"m\">L</span> is the largest curvature (the largest eigenvalue of the Hessian).</p>\n<p>The trouble comes when curvatures differ a lot. In the ravine <span class=\"m\">f = 0.2x^2 + 4y^2</span> the curvatures are <span class=\"m\">0.4</span> and <span class=\"m\">8</span>. Stability needs <span class=\"m\">\\eta &lt; 0.25</span>. With <span class=\"m\">\\eta = 0.2</span>, the <span class=\"m\">y</span> coordinate is multiplied by <span class=\"m\">1 - 1.6 = -0.6</span>, so it flips sign every step (a zig-zag across the valley), while <span class=\"m\">x</span> is multiplied by <span class=\"m\">1 - 0.08 = 0.92</span>, crawling slowly along the valley floor. The ratio of largest to smallest curvature, here <span class=\"m\">8/0.4 = 20</span>, is the <strong>condition number</strong> <span class=\"m\">\\kappa</span>. Large <span class=\"m\">\\kappa</span> means slow gradient descent, and the number of steps needed grows roughly in proportion to <span class=\"m\">\\kappa</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Condition number</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For a quadratic or a function near its minimum, the ratio <span class=\"m\">\\kappa = L/\\mu</span> of the largest to the smallest curvature (Hessian eigenvalue). Large <span class=\"m\">\\kappa</span> means long narrow valleys and slow gradient descent.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"a-guarantee-for-general-functions\" data-h=\"3\">A guarantee for general functions</h3>\n<p>Real losses are not quadratics, but a similar rule holds. A function is <strong><span class=\"m\">L</span>-smooth</strong> if its gradient never changes faster than <span class=\"m\">L</span> times the distance moved; for twice-differentiable functions this means the Hessian's eigenvalues are at most <span class=\"m\">L</span> in size. For such functions, a step with <span class=\"m\">\\eta = 1/L</span> always decreases the loss by at least <span class=\"m\">\\frac1{2L}\\|\\nabla f\\|^2</span>. This is the <strong>descent lemma</strong>. It guarantees steady progress until the gradient is small. For convex smooth functions the error after <span class=\"m\">t</span> steps shrinks like <span class=\"m\">1/t</span>, and for strongly convex functions it shrinks geometrically, by a factor of about <span class=\"m\">1 - 1/\\kappa</span> per step.</p>\n<h3 id=\"when-to-stop\" data-h=\"3\">When to stop</h3>\n<p>In practice you stop when the gradient norm is below a tolerance, when the loss stops improving, or after a fixed budget of steps. If you do not know <span class=\"m\">L</span>, a <strong>backtracking line search</strong> chooses each step automatically: start with a large <span class=\"m\">\\eta</span> and halve it until the loss decreases by a reasonable fraction of what the gradient predicts.</p>\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Adding the gradient instead of subtracting it, which climbs the hill. Picking one learning rate without testing a range on a log scale (such as <span class=\"m\">10^{-4}</span> to <span class=\"m\">1</span>). Blaming the algorithm when the real problem is badly scaled features: standardizing inputs reduces the condition number dramatically. Concluding that the minimum is reached just because the loss decreases slowly: a small gradient in a flat valley floor can still be far from the minimum.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Gradient descent steps along <span class=\"m\">-\\nabla f</span> with learning rate <span class=\"m\">\\eta</span>. On a quadratic with curvature <span class=\"m\">a</span> it converges exactly when <span class=\"m\">\\eta &lt; 2/a</span>, and in many dimensions the largest curvature sets that limit. Speed is governed by the condition number: narrow valleys cause zig-zags and slow progress. For <span class=\"m\">L</span>-smooth functions, <span class=\"m\">\\eta = 1/L</span> guarantees decrease.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"gd\" data-args='{&quot;surface&quot;: &quot;bowl&quot;, &quot;lr&quot;: 0.1}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> The bowl <span class=\"m\">f = x^2 + 2y^2</span>. Try learning rates 0.05, 0.1, 0.25, 0.45 and 0.55.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Press Run with <span class=\"m\">\\eta = 0.1</span> and count how many steps it takes to get close to the centre.\n2. Set <span class=\"m\">\\eta = 0.25</span>. The <span class=\"m\">y</span> factor <span class=\"m\">1 - 4\\eta</span> becomes 0, so the path drops to the <span class=\"m\">x</span>-axis in one step.\n3. Set <span class=\"m\">\\eta = 0.45</span>. The path zig-zags in <span class=\"m\">y</span> (factor <span class=\"m\">-0.8</span>) but still converges.\n4. Set <span class=\"m\">\\eta = 0.55</span> and run. The loss blows up, because <span class=\"m\">\\eta &gt; 2/4 = 0.5</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the threshold <span class=\"m\">\\eta &lt; 2/L</span> from the article is sharp, and the steepest direction (<span class=\"m\">y</span>, curvature 4) is the one that misbehaves first.</p></div></div>\n<figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"gd\" data-args='{&quot;surface&quot;: &quot;ravine&quot;, &quot;lr&quot;: 0.2}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> The ravine <span class=\"m\">f = 0.2x^2 + 4y^2</span> with condition number 20. Compare plain gradient descent with momentum.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Run plain GD with <span class=\"m\">\\eta = 0.2</span> and watch the zig-zag across the narrow direction.\n2. Lower <span class=\"m\">\\eta</span> to <span class=\"m\">0.05</span>. The zig-zag disappears, but progress along the valley floor becomes painfully slow.\n3. Raise <span class=\"m\">\\eta</span> to <span class=\"m\">0.26</span>. The path diverges, since <span class=\"m\">2/8 = 0.25</span>.\n4. Switch to Momentum and run again with <span class=\"m\">\\eta = 0.05</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> there is no learning rate that is good for both directions: the steep direction caps <span class=\"m\">\\eta</span>, and the flat direction then moves at the rate <span class=\"m\">1 - 0.4\\eta</span>. This is the condition-number problem, and it is what momentum and adaptive methods in later lessons are designed to fix.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\theta_{t+1} = \\theta_t - \\eta\\nabla f(\\theta_t)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">f = \\tfrac a2x^2:\\ x_{t+1} = (1 - \\eta a)x_t,\\quad\\text{converges iff }0 &lt; \\eta &lt; \\tfrac2a,\\ \\text{best }\\eta = \\tfrac1a</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{quadratic in }n\\text{-D: }\\eta &lt; \\frac2{\\lambda_{\\max}(H)},\\qquad\\kappa = \\frac{\\lambda_{\\max}}{\\lambda_{\\min}}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">L\\text{-smooth, }\\eta = \\tfrac1L:\\quad f(\\theta_{t+1})\\le f(\\theta_t) - \\tfrac1{2L}\\|\\nabla f(\\theta_t)\\|^2</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Steepest descent direction</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Among all unit vectors <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf u</span>, the directional derivative <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f^T\\mathbf u</span> is smallest for <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf u = -\\nabla f/\\|\\nabla f\\|</span>, where it equals <span class=\"m\">-\\|\\nabla f\\|</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-title\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>By Cauchy-Schwarz, <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f^T\\mathbf u\\ge-\\|\\nabla f\\|\\|\\mathbf u\\| = -\\|\\nabla f\\|</span>, with equality exactly when <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf u</span> is a negative multiple of <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Descent lemma</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f</span> is <span class=\"m\">L</span>-Lipschitz, then <span class=\"m\">f(\\mathbf y)\\le f(\\mathbf x) + \\nabla f(\\mathbf x)^T(\\mathbf y - \\mathbf x) + \\frac L2\\|\\mathbf y - \\mathbf x\\|^2</span>. With <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf y = \\mathbf x - \\frac1L\\nabla f(\\mathbf x)</span> this gives <span class=\"m\">f(\\mathbf y)\\le f(\\mathbf x) - \\frac1{2L}\\|\\nabla f(\\mathbf x)\\|^2</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-title\">Sketch</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Write <span class=\"m\">f(\\mathbf y) - f(\\mathbf x) = \\int_0^1\\nabla f(\\mathbf x + s(\\mathbf y - \\mathbf x))^T(\\mathbf y - \\mathbf x)\\,ds</span> and bound the difference between this gradient and <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f(\\mathbf x)</span> by <span class=\"m\">Ls\\|\\mathbf y - \\mathbf x\\|</span>; integrating <span class=\"m\">s</span> gives the <span class=\"m\">\\frac L2</span> term. Substituting the step and simplifying <span class=\"m\">-\\frac1L\\|\\mathbf g\\|^2 + \\frac1{2L}\\|\\mathbf g\\|^2</span> gives the second statement.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Gradient descent on <span class=\"m\">f(x) = x^2</span> from <span class=\"m\">x_0 = 4</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">f'(x) = 2x</span>, so with <span class=\"m\">\\eta = 0.25</span> the update is <span class=\"m\">x\\leftarrow0.5x</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">x_1 = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">x_2 = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">x_3 = 0.5</span>: the error halves each step.</li>\n<li>With <span class=\"m\">\\eta = 1</span>: <span class=\"m\">x\\leftarrow-x</span>, bouncing between <span class=\"m\">4</span> and <span class=\"m\">-4</span>. With <span class=\"m\">\\eta &gt; 1</span> the iterates grow: divergence.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">One step on the bowl <span class=\"m\">f = x^2 + 2y^2</span> from <span class=\"m\">(-2.5, 1.5)</span> with <span class=\"m\">\\eta = 0.1</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Gradient: <span class=\"m\">(2x, 4y) = (-5, 6)</span>.</li>\n<li>Step: <span class=\"m\">(-2.5, 1.5) - 0.1(-5, 6) = (-2, 0.9)</span>.</li>\n<li>Loss: from <span class=\"m\">6.25 + 2\\times2.25 = 10.75</span> down to <span class=\"m\">4 + 2\\times0.81 = 5.62</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Let <span class=\"m\">f(x) = 3x^2 - 6x + 5</span>. (a) Find the minimizer. (b) For which learning rates does gradient descent converge? (c) Starting from <span class=\"m\">x_0 = 3</span> with <span class=\"m\">\\eta = 0.1</span>, compute <span class=\"m\">x_1</span> and <span class=\"m\">x_2</span> and the factor by which the error shrinks per step. (d) Which <span class=\"m\">\\eta</span> reaches the minimum in one step?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) <span class=\"m\">f'(x) = 6x - 6 = 0</span> at <span class=\"m\">x = 1</span>.\n(b) The curvature is <span class=\"m\">a = f'' = 6</span>, so <span class=\"m\">0 &lt; \\eta &lt; 2/6 = 1/3</span>.\n(c) <span class=\"m\">x_1 = 3 - 0.1\\times12 = 1.8</span> and <span class=\"m\">x_2 = 1.8 - 0.1\\times4.8 = 1.32</span>. The errors are <span class=\"m\">2, 0.8, 0.32</span>, shrinking by <span class=\"m\">|1 - 0.6| = 0.4</span> each step.\n(d) <span class=\"m\">\\eta = 1/a = 1/6</span>: <span class=\"m\">x_1 = 3 - 12/6 = 1</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Every neural network is trained by some variant of gradient descent. Normalizing input features (zero mean, unit variance) and normalization layers inside networks improve the effective condition number, which is a large part of why they speed up training.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Gradient descent is an iterative numerical algorithm: each step costs one gradient evaluation, and the number of steps needed grows with the condition number and with the required accuracy. Analysing that trade-off is the same kind of reasoning as analysing an algorithm's running time.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A thermostat adjusting a heater by an amount proportional to the temperature error is doing gradient descent on the squared error. Turn the gain up too far and the room temperature overshoots and oscillates, exactly like a learning rate above <span class=\"m\">2/a</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Gradient descent on <span class=\"m\">f(x) = x^2</span> with <span class=\"m\">\\eta = 0.1</span> from <span class=\"m\">x = 10</span>. What is <span class=\"m\">x_1</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"8\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">x_1 = 10 - 0.1\\times20 = 8</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For <span class=\"m\">f(x) = 5x^2</span>, what is the largest learning rate for which gradient descent converges? (Give the boundary value.)</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0.2|1/5\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Curvature <span class=\"m\">a = 10</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\eta &lt; 2/10</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Gradient descent on <span class=\"m\">f(x) = x^2</span> with <span class=\"m\">\\eta = 0.5</span> from <span class=\"m\">x = 3</span>. What is <span class=\"m\">x_1</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\eta = 1/a</span> with <span class=\"m\">a = 2</span> jumps straight to the minimum.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the condition number of <span class=\"m\">f(x, y) = 0.5x^2 + 8y^2</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"16\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Curvatures are 1 and 16.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The direction of steepest descent at a point is...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">-\\nabla f</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\nabla f</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">perpendicular to <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The gradient points uphill; its negative points downhill most steeply.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">On a long narrow valley, plain gradient descent typically...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">zig-zags across the valley and moves slowly along it</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">converges in one step</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">moves only along the valley floor</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The steep direction limits the step, so the flat direction is slow.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "sgd": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-not-use-all-the-data\" data-h=\"3\">Why not use all the data?</h3>\n<p>The loss of a machine learning model is an average over the training set: <span class=\"m\">f(\\theta) = \\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n\\ell_i(\\theta)</span>, where <span class=\"m\">\\ell_i</span> is the loss on example <span class=\"m\">i</span>. Its gradient is the average of the per-example gradients. For a dataset of a million images, a single exact gradient requires a million forward and backward passes, and gradient descent needs thousands of gradients. That is far too slow.</p>\n<p>Imagine you want to know the average opinion of a country about a new policy. You do not ask all 60 million people; you poll a random sample of 1000 and accept a small margin of error. <strong>Stochastic gradient descent</strong> (SGD) applies the same idea to gradients: estimate the average gradient from a small random sample of examples, take a step, and repeat with a fresh sample.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-algorithm\" data-h=\"3\">The algorithm</h3>\n<p>Plain SGD picks one random example <span class=\"m\">i</span> and steps with its gradient: <span class=\"m\">\\theta\\leftarrow\\theta - \\eta\\nabla\\ell_i(\\theta)</span>. <strong>Mini-batch</strong> SGD picks a random batch <span class=\"m\">B</span> of examples and averages:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\theta\\leftarrow\\theta - \\eta\\,\\frac1{|B|}\\sum_{i\\in B}\\nabla\\ell_i(\\theta)</span>\nBatch sizes from 32 to a few thousand are typical. When people say \"SGD\" today they almost always mean the mini-batch version.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Stochastic gradient descent (SGD)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Gradient descent in which the full gradient is replaced by the gradient of the loss on a randomly chosen example or mini-batch, giving a cheap unbiased estimate of the true gradient.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Epoch</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>One full pass through the training set. With <span class=\"m\">n</span> examples and batch size <span class=\"m\">B</span>, an epoch contains <span class=\"m\">n/B</span> update steps.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"why-it-works-unbiased-but-noisy\" data-h=\"3\">Why it works: unbiased but noisy</h3>\n<p>If the batch is chosen at random, the mini-batch gradient equals the true gradient <strong>on average</strong>: it is an unbiased estimate. Here is a tiny check. Suppose we fit a constant <span class=\"m\">c</span> to the data <span class=\"m\">\\{1, 2, 6\\}</span> with <span class=\"m\">\\ell_i(c) = \\frac12(c - x_i)^2</span>. The per-example gradients are <span class=\"m\">c - x_i</span>. At <span class=\"m\">c = 0</span> they are <span class=\"m\">-1</span>, <span class=\"m\">-2</span> and <span class=\"m\">-6</span>, and their average, <span class=\"m\">-3</span>, is the full gradient <span class=\"m\">c - \\bar x</span>. A random single example gives <span class=\"m\">-1</span>, <span class=\"m\">-2</span> or <span class=\"m\">-6</span>, each with probability <span class=\"m\">\\frac13</span>: wrong individually, right on average. A batch of two, say <span class=\"m\">\\{1, 6\\}</span>, gives <span class=\"m\">-3.5</span>, closer.</p>\n<p>The estimate's spread shrinks with the batch size. If one example's gradient has standard deviation <span class=\"m\">\\sigma</span>, the average of <span class=\"m\">B</span> independent ones has standard deviation <span class=\"m\">\\sigma/\\sqrt B</span>. Halving the noise therefore requires four times the batch, which is the same square-root law that governs polling margins.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-noise-floor-and-learning-rate-schedules\" data-h=\"3\">The noise floor and learning-rate schedules</h3>\n<p>With a constant learning rate, SGD never settles exactly at the minimum. Close to the minimum the true gradient is tiny, but the noise is not, so the parameters keep jittering in a small region whose size is proportional to <span class=\"m\">\\eta</span>. To converge exactly, the learning rate must shrink over time. A classical sufficient condition (Robbins and Monro) is\n<span class=\"mb\">\\sum_t\\eta_t = \\infty\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\\sum_t\\eta_t^2 &lt; \\infty</span>\nwhich holds for <span class=\"m\">\\eta_t = \\eta_0/t</span>. The first condition means the steps can travel any distance; the second means the noise they add eventually dies out.</p>\n<p>In deep learning people use <strong>schedules</strong> that keep the rate high early (fast progress) and lower it later (fine settling). <strong>Step decay</strong> divides <span class=\"m\">\\eta</span> by 10 at fixed epochs. <strong>Cosine decay</strong> follows <span class=\"m\">\\eta_t = \\eta_{\\min} + \\frac12(\\eta_{\\max} - \\eta_{\\min})\\left(1 + \\cos\\frac{\\pi t}T\\right)</span> from <span class=\"m\">\\eta_{\\max}</span> at <span class=\"m\">t = 0</span> down to <span class=\"m\">\\eta_{\\min}</span> at <span class=\"m\">t = T</span>. <strong>Warmup</strong> ramps <span class=\"m\">\\eta</span> up from near 0 over the first few hundred or thousand steps, which avoids instability while the network's statistics settle.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Learning-rate schedule</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A rule <span class=\"m\">\\eta_t</span> that changes the learning rate during training, such as step decay, cosine decay or linear warmup.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"choosing-the-batch-size\" data-h=\"3\">Choosing the batch size</h3>\n<p>Bigger batches give less noisy gradients and use parallel hardware efficiently, but each example contributes less to progress, and very large batches can generalize slightly worse. A useful heuristic is the <strong>linear scaling rule</strong>: if you multiply the batch size by <span class=\"m\">k</span>, multiply the learning rate by <span class=\"m\">k</span> too (with warmup), because the averaged gradient is less noisy and can tolerate a larger step. Small batches add noise that can actually help: it pushes the parameters out of saddle points and sharp, narrow minima.</p>\n<h3 id=\"epochs-and-shuffling\" data-h=\"3\">Epochs and shuffling</h3>\n<p>Rather than sampling with replacement, practitioners shuffle the dataset at the start of each epoch and walk through it in batches. With <span class=\"m\">n = 50{,}000</span> examples and <span class=\"m\">B = 100</span>, one epoch is 500 steps. Shuffling every epoch matters: if the data were sorted, for example by class, each batch would push the model in a biased direction.</p>\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Not shuffling the data, so batches are not representative. Judging progress from the loss of a single mini-batch, which is noisy; average over many batches. Changing the batch size without retuning the learning rate. Expecting a constant-learning-rate run to converge exactly instead of hovering in a noise ball.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>SGD replaces the full gradient with the average gradient of a random mini-batch: unbiased, cheap and noisy, with standard deviation <span class=\"m\">\\sigma/\\sqrt B</span>. An epoch is one pass through the data. Because of the noise, the learning rate must decrease over time, either by the Robbins-Monro conditions or by practical schedules such as cosine decay with warmup. Batch size trades noise against hardware efficiency, roughly following the linear scaling rule.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"lln\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Each run averages more and more random samples. A mini-batch gradient is such an average, and its error shrinks as the batch grows.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Press Flip 1000 × 5 runs and look at how far the five running averages are from the true value after 10 flips, then after 1000.\n2. Estimate the spread after about 25 flips and after about 100 flips. It should roughly halve, since <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{100/25} = 2</span>.\n3. Move the true probability toward 0 or 1 and repeat. The spread shrinks, because each sample has less variance.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> each run's average is an unbiased estimate that becomes accurate like <span class=\"m\">1/\\sqrt n</span>. A mini-batch gradient behaves the same way, with the batch size playing the role of the number of flips. That is the <span class=\"m\">\\sigma/\\sqrt B</span> rule from the article, and why quadrupling the batch only halves the noise.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">f(\\theta) = \\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n\\ell_i(\\theta),\\qquad\\mathbf g_B = \\frac1{|B|}\\sum_{i\\in B}\\nabla\\ell_i(\\theta),\\qquad\\mathbb E[\\mathbf g_B] = \\nabla f(\\theta)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\operatorname{sd}(\\mathbf g_B) = \\frac\\sigma{\\sqrt B}\\qquad\\text{steps per epoch} = \\frac nB</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\sum_t\\eta_t = \\infty,\\quad\\sum_t\\eta_t^2 &lt; \\infty\\qquad\\text{(e.g. }\\eta_t = \\eta_0/t\\text{)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\eta_t = \\eta_{\\min} + \\tfrac12(\\eta_{\\max} - \\eta_{\\min})\\left(1 + \\cos\\tfrac{\\pi t}{T}\\right)</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Mini-batch gradients are unbiased with variance <span class=\"m\">\\sigma^2/B</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If the <span class=\"m\">B</span> indices are drawn independently and uniformly, then <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb E[\\mathbf g_B] = \\nabla f(\\theta)</span> and, coordinatewise, <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Var}(\\mathbf g_B) = \\sigma^2/B</span>, where <span class=\"m\">\\sigma^2</span> is the variance of a single example's gradient.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-title\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Each sampled gradient has mean <span class=\"m\">\\frac1n\\sum_i\\nabla\\ell_i = \\nabla f</span>, so their average has the same mean by linearity of expectation. The samples are independent, so the variance of their sum is <span class=\"m\">B\\sigma^2</span>, and dividing by <span class=\"m\">B</span> multiplies the variance by <span class=\"m\">1/B^2</span>, giving <span class=\"m\">\\sigma^2/B</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Robbins-Monro convergence (informal)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For a strongly convex smooth objective with bounded gradient noise, SGD with step sizes satisfying <span class=\"m\">\\sum_t\\eta_t = \\infty</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\sum_t\\eta_t^2 &lt; \\infty</span> converges to the minimizer with probability 1.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Per-example and mini-batch gradients for fitting <span class=\"m\">c</span> to <span class=\"m\">\\{1, 2, 6\\}</span> at <span class=\"m\">c = 0</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Per-example gradients <span class=\"m\">c - x_i</span>: <span class=\"m\">-1</span>, <span class=\"m\">-2</span>, <span class=\"m\">-6</span>.</li>\n<li>Full gradient: their average, <span class=\"m\">-3</span>, which equals <span class=\"m\">c - \\bar x</span>.</li>\n<li>Batch <span class=\"m\">\\{1, 6\\}</span>: <span class=\"m\">-3.5</span>; batch <span class=\"m\">\\{1, 2\\}</span>: <span class=\"m\">-1.5</span>. Averaged over all three possible pairs: <span class=\"m\">(-1.5 - 3.5 - 4)/3 = -3</span>. Unbiased.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">How big a batch to cut the noise?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Suppose a single example's gradient has standard deviation <span class=\"m\">\\sigma = 2</span>.</li>\n<li>With <span class=\"m\">B = 16</span>, the batch gradient has standard deviation <span class=\"m\">2/\\sqrt{16} = 0.5</span>.</li>\n<li>To reach <span class=\"m\">0.25</span> you need <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt B = 8</span>, so <span class=\"m\">B = 64</span>: four times the batch for half the noise.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A dataset has 60,000 examples and you train with batch size 200 for 10 epochs. (a) How many update steps is that? (b) If a single-example gradient coordinate has standard deviation 3, what is the standard deviation of the batch estimate? (c) You switch to batch size 800. By the linear scaling rule, what learning rate should replace <span class=\"m\">\\eta = 0.01</span>? (d) With cosine decay from <span class=\"m\">\\eta_{\\max} = 0.04</span> to <span class=\"m\">\\eta_{\\min} = 0</span> over the whole run, what is the rate halfway through?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) <span class=\"m\">60000/200 = 300</span> steps per epoch, so 3000 steps.\n(b) <span class=\"m\">3/\\sqrt{200}\\approx0.212</span>.\n(c) The batch grew by a factor of 4, so <span class=\"m\">\\eta = 0.04</span>.\n(d) At <span class=\"m\">t = T/2</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\cos\\frac\\pi2 = 0</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\eta = \\frac12(0.04 + 0) = 0.02</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Essentially all deep networks are trained with mini-batch methods. When a desired batch does not fit in GPU memory, <strong>gradient accumulation</strong> sums the gradients of several smaller batches before one update, giving the same mean gradient. Large language models use warmup followed by cosine or linear decay.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>SGD is an online algorithm: it processes data in a stream and needs memory only for the current batch, so it scales to datasets far larger than memory. Data loaders shuffle with a buffer and prefetch batches in parallel with computation.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Opinion polls work on the same principle. A random sample of 1000 people estimates a national percentage with a margin of error of about 3 points, and quadrupling the sample to 4000 only halves that margin, the same <span class=\"m\">1/\\sqrt B</span> law that governs mini-batch noise.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Doubling the batch size divides the standard deviation of the gradient estimate by about...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">\\sqrt2</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">2</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">4</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Standard deviation scales like <span class=\"m\">1/\\sqrt B</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A dataset has 1000 examples and the batch size is 50. How many steps are in one epoch?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"20\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">1000/50 = 20</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Single-example gradients have standard deviation 4. What is the standard deviation of a batch of 16?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">4/\\sqrt{16} = 1</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which step-size sequence satisfies the Robbins-Monro conditions?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">\\eta_t = 1/t</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\eta_t = 0.1</span> for all <span class=\"m\">t</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\eta_t = 1/t^2</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\sum1/t</span> diverges and <span class=\"m\">\\sum1/t^2</span> converges. A constant fails the second condition, and <span class=\"m\">1/t^2</span> fails the first.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">With cosine decay from <span class=\"m\">\\eta_{\\max}</span> to <span class=\"m\">\\eta_{\\min}</span>, what is the rate at the final step <span class=\"m\">t = T</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">\\eta_{\\min}</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\eta_{\\max}</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">(\\eta_{\\max} + \\eta_{\\min})/2</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\cos\\pi = -1</span>, so the bracket is 0.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Why does SGD with a constant learning rate not settle exactly at the minimum?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">The gradient noise keeps moving the parameters even where the true gradient is zero.</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">The true gradient is biased.</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">It always diverges.</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Near the minimum the noise dominates, so the iterates jitter in a region whose size scales with <span class=\"m\">\\eta</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "adam": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"the-problem-with-plain-gradient-descent\" data-h=\"3\">The problem with plain gradient descent</h3>\n<p>In the last two lessons we saw that plain gradient descent struggles in long narrow valleys: the steep direction forces a small learning rate, which leaves the gentle direction crawling, and the path zig-zags. Deep learning losses are full of such valleys, and different parameters can have gradients that differ in size by factors of a thousand. The optimizers in this lesson fix both problems with two simple ideas: <strong>remember</strong> the recent gradients, and <strong>rescale</strong> each parameter's step by how large its gradients usually are.</p>\n<p>Imagine pushing a heavy shopping trolley down a bumpy, slightly sloping car park. Small bumps knock it left and right, but its weight smooths them out, and on a steady slope it gathers speed. That is momentum. Now imagine each wheel has its own suspension that stiffens on rough ground and loosens on smooth ground. That is the per-parameter adaptation of RMSProp. Adam is a trolley with both.</p>\n<h3 id=\"momentum\" data-h=\"3\">Momentum</h3>\n<p>Momentum keeps a <strong>velocity</strong> <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf v</span>, a running sum of past gradients that slowly forgets:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\mathbf v\\leftarrow\\beta\\mathbf v + \\mathbf g,\\qquad\\theta\\leftarrow\\theta - \\eta\\mathbf v</span>\nwhere <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf g</span> is the current gradient and <span class=\"m\">\\beta</span> (typically <span class=\"m\">0.9</span>) controls the memory. If the gradient keeps pointing the same way, the velocity grows. With <span class=\"m\">g = 1</span> every step and <span class=\"m\">\\beta = 0.9</span>, the velocity goes <span class=\"m\">1, 1.9, 2.71, 3.44, \\ldots</span> and approaches <span class=\"m\">1/(1 - \\beta) = 10</span>. So along a consistent slope the effective learning rate is ten times larger. If the gradient alternates, <span class=\"m\">+1, -1, +1, \\ldots</span>, the velocity goes <span class=\"m\">1, -0.1, 0.91, -0.18, \\ldots</span>, staying small because the contributions cancel. In a ravine, the zig-zag component across the valley alternates and is damped, while the steady component along the valley accumulates. That is exactly what we wanted.</p>\n<p>A popular variant, <strong>Nesterov momentum</strong>, evaluates the gradient at the look-ahead point <span class=\"m\">\\theta - \\eta\\beta\\mathbf v</span> instead of at <span class=\"m\">\\theta</span>, which corrects overshoots slightly earlier.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Momentum</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>An optimizer that keeps an exponentially decaying sum of past gradients, <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf v\\leftarrow\\beta\\mathbf v + \\mathbf g</span>, and steps along it, <span class=\"m\">\\theta\\leftarrow\\theta - \\eta\\mathbf v</span>. It accelerates along consistent directions and damps oscillations.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"rmsprop-a-step-size-for-every-parameter\" data-h=\"3\">RMSProp: a step size for every parameter</h3>\n<p>Suppose one weight always has gradients near <span class=\"m\">100</span> and another near <span class=\"m\">0.01</span>. A single learning rate is too big for the first or too small for the second. RMSProp keeps a running average of each parameter's <strong>squared</strong> gradient and divides by its square root:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\mathbf s\\leftarrow\\rho\\mathbf s + (1 - \\rho)\\mathbf g^2,\\qquad\\theta\\leftarrow\\theta - \\eta\\frac{\\mathbf g}{\\sqrt{\\mathbf s} + \\epsilon}</span>\nAll operations are elementwise, and <span class=\"m\">\\epsilon</span> (such as <span class=\"m\">10^{-8}</span>) prevents division by zero. Dividing a gradient by its typical size makes the step roughly <span class=\"m\">\\eta</span> for both weights in the example, whatever the scale of their gradients. In a ravine, the steep direction has large squared gradients and so gets a smaller step, while the gentle direction gets a larger one, which reduces the condition-number problem.</p>\n<h3 id=\"adam-momentum-plus-rmsprop\" data-h=\"3\">Adam: momentum plus RMSProp</h3>\n<p>Adam (adaptive moment estimation) keeps both a running mean of gradients <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf m</span> (the first moment) and a running mean of squared gradients <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf v</span> (the second moment):\n<span class=\"mb\">\\mathbf m\\leftarrow\\beta_1\\mathbf m + (1 - \\beta_1)\\mathbf g,\\qquad\\mathbf v\\leftarrow\\beta_2\\mathbf v + (1 - \\beta_2)\\mathbf g^2</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\hat{\\mathbf m} = \\frac{\\mathbf m}{1 - \\beta_1^t},\\qquad\\hat{\\mathbf v} = \\frac{\\mathbf v}{1 - \\beta_2^t},\\qquad\\theta\\leftarrow\\theta - \\eta\\frac{\\hat{\\mathbf m}}{\\sqrt{\\hat{\\mathbf v}} + \\epsilon}</span>\nThe defaults <span class=\"m\">\\beta_1 = 0.9</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\beta_2 = 0.999</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\epsilon = 10^{-8}</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\eta = 10^{-3}</span> work surprisingly often.</p>\n<p>The hats are <strong>bias corrections</strong>. Both averages start at zero, so early on they are too small. After the first step with gradient <span class=\"m\">g = 4</span>: <span class=\"m\">m = 0.1\\times4 = 0.4</span> and <span class=\"m\">v = 0.001\\times16 = 0.016</span>. Dividing by <span class=\"m\">1 - 0.9 = 0.1</span> and <span class=\"m\">1 - 0.999 = 0.001</span> gives <span class=\"m\">\\hat m = 4</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\hat v = 16</span>, the right values. The first step is then <span class=\"m\">\\eta\\cdot4/\\sqrt{16} = \\eta</span>. In fact Adam's first step is <span class=\"m\">\\eta</span> times the sign of the gradient for every parameter, and later steps are typically no larger than about <span class=\"m\">\\eta</span>. This makes the learning rate a natural \"how far should each weight move per step\" setting.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Adam</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>An optimizer that maintains bias-corrected exponential moving averages of the gradient and the squared gradient for each parameter, and steps by <span class=\"m\">\\eta\\,\\hat{\\mathbf m}/(\\sqrt{\\hat{\\mathbf v}} + \\epsilon)</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"weight-decay-and-adamw\" data-h=\"3\">Weight decay and AdamW</h3>\n<p>A common regularizer is the L2 penalty <span class=\"m\">\\frac\\lambda2\\|\\theta\\|^2</span>, whose gradient is <span class=\"m\">\\lambda\\theta</span>. In plain SGD, adding it gives <span class=\"m\">\\theta\\leftarrow\\theta - \\eta(\\mathbf g + \\lambda\\theta) = (1 - \\eta\\lambda)\\theta - \\eta\\mathbf g</span>: the weights shrink by a constant factor every step, which is why it is called <strong>weight decay</strong>. In Adam, however, adding <span class=\"m\">\\lambda\\theta</span> to the gradient sends it through the division by <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{\\hat{\\mathbf v}}</span>, so weights with large gradients are barely decayed at all. <strong>AdamW</strong> fixes this by applying the decay directly to the weights, separately from the adaptive step:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\theta\\leftarrow\\theta - \\eta\\left(\\frac{\\hat{\\mathbf m}}{\\sqrt{\\hat{\\mathbf v}} + \\epsilon} + \\lambda\\theta\\right)</span>\nThis \"decoupled\" weight decay regularizes every weight equally and is the default for training transformers.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Weight decay</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Shrinking the weights toward zero by a small fraction each step, <span class=\"m\">\\theta\\leftarrow(1 - \\eta\\lambda)\\theta</span>, as a regularizer. In SGD it is equivalent to an L2 penalty; in adaptive methods it is not, which is why AdamW applies it separately.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"which-optimizer-should-you-use\" data-h=\"3\">Which optimizer should you use?</h3>\n<p>Adam or AdamW is the usual default because it needs little tuning and handles parameters of very different scales. SGD with momentum is still popular for image classifiers, where it sometimes generalizes slightly better with a well-tuned schedule. Both still need a learning-rate schedule and warmup.</p>\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Forgetting bias correction, which makes the first steps much too large because <span class=\"m\">\\hat{\\mathbf v}</span> is underestimated more than <span class=\"m\">\\hat{\\mathbf m}</span>. Using Adam's L2 penalty and expecting it to behave like weight decay; use AdamW. Reusing SGD's learning rate (such as <span class=\"m\">0.1</span>) for Adam, whose steps are about <span class=\"m\">\\eta</span> per weight and which therefore needs something like <span class=\"m\">10^{-3}</span>. Thinking momentum <span class=\"m\">\\beta = 0.99</span> is \"a little more\" than <span class=\"m\">0.9</span>: it multiplies the effective step by 100 instead of 10.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Momentum averages gradients over time, speeding up consistent directions and damping oscillations, with an effective step of <span class=\"m\">\\eta/(1 - \\beta)</span> on steady slopes. RMSProp divides by the root mean square of recent gradients, giving each parameter its own scale. Adam combines both with bias correction, so each step moves each weight by roughly <span class=\"m\">\\eta</span>. AdamW decouples weight decay from the adaptive step.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"gd\" data-args='{&quot;surface&quot;: &quot;rosen&quot;, &quot;lr&quot;: 0.002}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> The Rosenbrock banana valley, with its minimum at <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span>. Compare Plain GD, Momentum and Adam from the same start.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Run Plain GD with <span class=\"m\">\\eta = 0.002</span> for 150 steps and note the final position and loss.\n2. Reset, choose Momentum and run with the same <span class=\"m\">\\eta</span>. Compare how far along the curved valley it gets.\n3. Choose Adam. Its steps are about <span class=\"m\">\\eta</span> in each coordinate, so raise <span class=\"m\">\\eta</span> to around <span class=\"m\">0.02</span> to <span class=\"m\">0.05</span> and run.\n4. Drag the start point to the other side of the valley and repeat the comparison.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> plain GD reaches the valley floor quickly and then crawls along it, the condition-number problem from the article. Momentum builds speed along the floor, and Adam rescales each coordinate so that the gentle direction no longer moves at a crawl.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{Momentum: }\\mathbf v\\leftarrow\\beta\\mathbf v + \\mathbf g,\\quad\\theta\\leftarrow\\theta - \\eta\\mathbf v</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{RMSProp: }\\mathbf s\\leftarrow\\rho\\mathbf s + (1 - \\rho)\\mathbf g^2,\\quad\\theta\\leftarrow\\theta - \\eta\\,\\mathbf g/(\\sqrt{\\mathbf s} + \\epsilon)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Adam: }\\mathbf m\\leftarrow\\beta_1\\mathbf m + (1 - \\beta_1)\\mathbf g,\\ \\mathbf v\\leftarrow\\beta_2\\mathbf v + (1 - \\beta_2)\\mathbf g^2,\\ \\hat{\\mathbf m} = \\tfrac{\\mathbf m}{1 - \\beta_1^t},\\ \\hat{\\mathbf v} = \\tfrac{\\mathbf v}{1 - \\beta_2^t}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\theta\\leftarrow\\theta - \\eta\\frac{\\hat{\\mathbf m}}{\\sqrt{\\hat{\\mathbf v}} + \\epsilon}\\qquad\\text{AdamW: add }-\\eta\\lambda\\theta\\text{ separately}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Momentum on a constant gradient</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf g</span> is constant and <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf v_0 = \\mathbf 0</span>, then <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf v_t = \\frac{1 - \\beta^t}{1 - \\beta}\\mathbf g\\to\\frac{\\mathbf g}{1 - \\beta}</span>, so the effective learning rate approaches <span class=\"m\">\\eta/(1 - \\beta)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-title\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Unrolling, <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf v_t = \\mathbf g(1 + \\beta + \\cdots + \\beta^{t-1})</span>, a geometric series with sum <span class=\"m\">\\frac{1 - \\beta^t}{1 - \\beta}</span>. For <span class=\"m\">0\\le\\beta &lt; 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\beta^t\\to0</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Adam's bias correction</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If the gradients have constant mean <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb E[\\mathbf g_t] = \\mu</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf m_0 = \\mathbf 0</span>, then <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb E[\\mathbf m_t] = (1 - \\beta_1^t)\\mu</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb E[\\hat{\\mathbf m}_t] = \\mu</span>. The same holds for <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf v_t</span> and the mean squared gradient.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-title\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\mathbf m_t = (1 - \\beta_1)\\sum_{k=1}^t\\beta_1^{t-k}\\mathbf g_k</span>. Taking expectations, <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb E[\\mathbf m_t] = (1 - \\beta_1)\\mu\\sum_{j=0}^{t-1}\\beta_1^j = (1 - \\beta_1^t)\\mu</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Momentum velocities with <span class=\"m\">\\beta = 0.9</span> and a constant gradient <span class=\"m\">g = 1</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">v_1 = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">v_2 = 0.9 + 1 = 1.9</span>, <span class=\"m\">v_3 = 1.71 + 1 = 2.71</span>.</li>\n<li>By the theorem, <span class=\"m\">v_t = 10(1 - 0.9^t)</span>; check <span class=\"m\">v_3 = 10(1 - 0.729) = 2.71</span>.</li>\n<li>In the long run the step is <span class=\"m\">10\\eta</span>: momentum <span class=\"m\">0.9</span> acts like a tenfold learning rate on steady slopes.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Adam's first step for two very different gradients, <span class=\"m\">g = 4</span> and <span class=\"m\">g = 0.01</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>For <span class=\"m\">g = 4</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\hat m = 4</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\hat v = 16</span>, step <span class=\"m\">\\eta\\cdot4/4 = \\eta</span>.</li>\n<li>For <span class=\"m\">g = 0.01</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\hat m = 0.01</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\hat v = 0.0001</span>, step <span class=\"m\">\\eta\\cdot0.01/0.01 = \\eta</span> (ignoring <span class=\"m\">\\epsilon</span>).</li>\n<li>Both weights move by <span class=\"m\">\\eta</span> in the downhill direction: Adam's first step is <span class=\"m\">\\eta</span> times the sign of the gradient.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Show that L2 regularization in SGD is weight decay</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Loss with penalty: <span class=\"m\">f(\\theta) + \\frac\\lambda2\\|\\theta\\|^2</span>, gradient <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf g + \\lambda\\theta</span>.</li>\n<li>SGD step: <span class=\"m\">\\theta - \\eta(\\mathbf g + \\lambda\\theta) = (1 - \\eta\\lambda)\\theta - \\eta\\mathbf g</span>.</li>\n<li>With <span class=\"m\">\\eta = 0.1</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = 0.01</span> the weights are multiplied by <span class=\"m\">0.999</span> every step before the gradient update.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A single parameter starts at <span class=\"m\">\\theta_0 = 1</span> and receives gradient <span class=\"m\">g = 2</span> on each of the first two steps. Use Adam with <span class=\"m\">\\beta_1 = 0.9</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\beta_2 = 0.999</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\eta = 0.001</span> and ignore <span class=\"m\">\\epsilon</span>. (a) Compute <span class=\"m\">m</span>, <span class=\"m\">v</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\hat m</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\hat v</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\theta</span> after each step. (b) What would the first step be without bias correction?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) Step 1: <span class=\"m\">m = 0.2</span>, <span class=\"m\">v = 0.004</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\hat m = 0.2/0.1 = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\hat v = 0.004/0.001 = 4</span>, step <span class=\"m\">0.001\\cdot2/2 = 0.001</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\theta_1 = 0.999</span>.\nStep 2: <span class=\"m\">m = 0.18 + 0.2 = 0.38</span>, <span class=\"m\">v = 0.003996 + 0.004 = 0.007996</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\hat m = 0.38/0.19 = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\hat v = 0.007996/0.001999 = 4</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\theta_2 = 0.998</span>.\n(b) <span class=\"m\">0.001\\cdot0.2/\\sqrt{0.004}\\approx0.001\\cdot0.2/0.0632\\approx0.00316</span>, more than three times too large, because the uncorrected second moment is far too small.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>AdamW with warmup and cosine decay trains almost every transformer; large language models often use <span class=\"m\">\\beta_2 = 0.95</span> for extra stability. SGD with momentum <span class=\"m\">0.9</span> remains standard for many convolutional networks. Learning-rate warmup is especially important for Adam, because <span class=\"m\">\\hat{\\mathbf v}</span> is a poor estimate in the first steps.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Adam stores two extra numbers per parameter. For a 7-billion-parameter model in 32-bit floats that is <span class=\"m\">2\\times7\\times10^9\\times4</span> bytes, about 56 GB just for optimizer state, which is why memory-saving optimizers and sharded optimizer states are active engineering topics.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Financial charts use exponential moving averages, exactly the update <span class=\"m\">m\\leftarrow\\beta m + (1 - \\beta)g</span>, to smooth noisy daily prices and reveal the trend. Traders face the same trade-off as momentum: a larger <span class=\"m\">\\beta</span> smooths more but reacts more slowly when the trend changes.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">In Adam, <span class=\"m\">\\beta_1</span> controls the averaging of...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">gradients (the first moment)</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">squared gradients</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the learning rate</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\beta_2</span> controls the squared gradients.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">With momentum <span class=\"m\">\\beta = 0.9</span> and a constant gradient, the long-run step is how many times <span class=\"m\">\\eta g</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"10\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">1/(1 - \\beta) = 10</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Momentum with <span class=\"m\">\\beta = 0.5</span> and gradient <span class=\"m\">g = 1</span> on both of the first two steps, starting from <span class=\"m\">v = 0</span>. What is <span class=\"m\">v</span> after two steps?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1.5|3/2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">v_1 = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">v_2 = 0.5 + 1 = 1.5</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Ignoring <span class=\"m\">\\epsilon</span>, what is the size of Adam's first step when <span class=\"m\">\\eta = 0.01</span> and <span class=\"m\">g = -3</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0.01|1/100\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The first step is <span class=\"m\">\\eta</span> times the sign of the gradient, whatever its size.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What does RMSProp do to a parameter whose gradients are consistently large?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">Gives it a smaller effective step</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Gives it a larger effective step</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Freezes it</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>It divides by the root mean square of recent gradients.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Why was AdamW introduced?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">Adding an L2 term to Adam's gradient gets rescaled by <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{\\hat{\\mathbf v}}</span>, so decay is uneven; AdamW applies it directly.</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Adam cannot be used with mini-batches.</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Adam has no learning rate.</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Decoupled weight decay shrinks every weight by the same factor <span class=\"m\">1 - \\eta\\lambda</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "newton": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"using-the-curvature\" data-h=\"3\">Using the curvature</h3>\n<p>Gradient descent only knows the slope, so it must guess how far to step; that guess is the learning rate. Suppose you also knew how quickly the slope changes, the curvature. Then you could fit a parabola to the function at your current point and jump straight to the parabola's lowest point. That is Newton's method. Where it works, it is astonishingly fast: the number of correct digits roughly doubles every step.</p>\n<p>Imagine sliding a hockey puck toward a target. If you only know the direction, you push and hope. If you also know how quickly the ice slows the puck, you can compute exactly how hard to push. Curvature plays the role of that extra knowledge.</p>\n<h3 id=\"newton-s-method-for-roots\" data-h=\"3\">Newton's method for roots</h3>\n<p>Newton's method was first a way to solve equations <span class=\"m\">f(x) = 0</span>. At the current guess <span class=\"m\">x_n</span>, replace <span class=\"m\">f</span> by its tangent line <span class=\"m\">f(x_n) + f'(x_n)(x - x_n)</span> and find where that line crosses zero:\n<span class=\"mb\">x_{n+1} = x_n - \\frac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}</span>\nFor <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt2</span>, solve <span class=\"m\">x^2 - 2 = 0</span>, so the update is <span class=\"m\">x\\leftarrow x - \\frac{x^2 - 2}{2x}</span>. From <span class=\"m\">x_0 = 1</span>: <span class=\"m\">x_1 = 1.5</span>, <span class=\"m\">x_2 = 1.41667</span>, <span class=\"m\">x_3 = 1.4142157</span>. The true value is <span class=\"m\">1.4142136</span>. The errors are about <span class=\"m\">0.4</span>, <span class=\"m\">0.09</span>, <span class=\"m\">0.0025</span> and <span class=\"m\">0.000002</span>: each is roughly the square of the one before (times a constant). This is <strong>quadratic convergence</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Quadratic convergence</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A sequence converges quadratically to <span class=\"m\">x^*</span> if <span class=\"m\">|x_{n+1} - x^*|\\le C|x_n - x^*|^2</span> for some constant <span class=\"m\">C</span> once <span class=\"m\">x_n</span> is close enough. Roughly, the number of correct digits doubles every step.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"newton-s-method-for-minimization\" data-h=\"3\">Newton's method for minimization</h3>\n<p>To minimize <span class=\"m\">f</span>, find a root of <span class=\"m\">f'</span>. Applying the same idea to <span class=\"m\">f'</span> gives\n<span class=\"mb\">x_{n+1} = x_n - \\frac{f'(x_n)}{f''(x_n)}</span>\nwhich is exactly the bottom of the parabola <span class=\"m\">f(x_n) + f'(x_n)(x - x_n) + \\frac12f''(x_n)(x - x_n)^2</span>. Try <span class=\"m\">f(x) = x - \\ln x</span> for <span class=\"m\">x &gt; 0</span>, whose minimum is at <span class=\"m\">x = 1</span>. Here <span class=\"m\">f'(x) = 1 - 1/x</span> and <span class=\"m\">f''(x) = 1/x^2</span>, so the update simplifies to <span class=\"m\">x\\leftarrow2x - x^2</span>. From <span class=\"m\">x_0 = 0.5</span>: <span class=\"m\">0.75</span>, <span class=\"m\">0.9375</span>, <span class=\"m\">0.99609</span>. The errors <span class=\"m\">0.5</span>, <span class=\"m\">0.25</span>, <span class=\"m\">0.0625</span>, <span class=\"m\">0.0039</span> are each exactly the square of the previous one, because <span class=\"m\">1 - (2x - x^2) = (1 - x)^2</span>.</p>\n<p>Compare this with gradient descent. Newton's step is gradient descent with learning rate <span class=\"m\">1/f''(x)</span>: the curvature picks the step size automatically. That is why, for a quadratic, Newton's method lands on the minimum in a single step from anywhere.</p>\n<h3 id=\"many-dimensions\" data-h=\"3\">Many dimensions</h3>\n<p>In <span class=\"m\">n</span> dimensions the curvature is the Hessian matrix <span class=\"m\">H = \\nabla^2f</span>, and the parabola becomes the quadratic model <span class=\"m\">f(\\theta) + \\nabla f^T\\mathbf d + \\frac12\\mathbf d^TH\\mathbf d</span>. Setting its gradient to zero gives the <strong>Newton step</strong>:\n<span class=\"mb\">H\\mathbf d = -\\nabla f,\\qquad\\theta\\leftarrow\\theta + \\mathbf d</span>\nIn practice you solve this linear system rather than computing <span class=\"m\">H^{-1}</span>. Because Newton's method rescales every direction by its own curvature, a long narrow valley is no harder than a round bowl: the condition number that slowed gradient descent no longer matters.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Newton step</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The step <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf d</span> solving <span class=\"m\">\\nabla^2f(\\theta)\\,\\mathbf d = -\\nabla f(\\theta)</span>; it moves to the minimum of the local quadratic model of <span class=\"m\">f</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"why-not-always-use-newton\" data-h=\"3\">Why not always use Newton?</h3>\n<p>Three problems. First, <strong>cost</strong>: for <span class=\"m\">n</span> parameters the Hessian has <span class=\"m\">n^2</span> entries and solving the system takes about <span class=\"m\">n^3</span> operations. With a million parameters that is <span class=\"m\">10^{12}</span> entries, about 8 terabytes in double precision. Second, <strong>direction</strong>: if the Hessian is not positive definite, as at a saddle point or far from a minimum of a non-convex function, the \"bottom of the parabola\" may be a maximum or a saddle, and the step can go uphill. Third, <strong>distance</strong>: far from the minimum the quadratic model may be poor and the full step may overshoot wildly. Practical versions add a line search, or use <span class=\"m\">(H + \\lambda I)\\mathbf d = -\\nabla f</span> (the Levenberg-Marquardt idea), which blends Newton with gradient descent.</p>\n<h3 id=\"quasi-newton-methods\" data-h=\"3\">Quasi-Newton methods</h3>\n<p><strong>Quasi-Newton</strong> methods keep most of Newton's speed without ever forming the Hessian. They watch how the gradient changes between steps. If the step was <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf s = \\theta_{t+1} - \\theta_t</span> and the gradient changed by <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf y = \\nabla f_{t+1} - \\nabla f_t</span>, then the true Hessian approximately satisfies <span class=\"m\">H\\mathbf s\\approx\\mathbf y</span> (the <strong>secant condition</strong>). <strong>BFGS</strong> updates an approximation of <span class=\"m\">H^{-1}</span> to satisfy it exactly at each step. <strong>L-BFGS</strong> stores only the last few pairs <span class=\"m\">(\\mathbf s, \\mathbf y)</span>, typically 5 to 20, so its memory is linear in <span class=\"m\">n</span>. For least-squares problems, the <strong>Gauss-Newton</strong> method uses <span class=\"m\">J^TJ</span>, built from the Jacobian of the residuals, as a positive semidefinite stand-in for the Hessian.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Quasi-Newton method</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>An optimization method that builds an approximation of the Hessian (or its inverse) from successive gradient differences, such as BFGS and L-BFGS, achieving fast convergence without computing second derivatives.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Computing <span class=\"m\">H^{-1}</span> explicitly instead of solving <span class=\"m\">H\\mathbf d = -\\nabla f</span>. Taking full Newton steps far from the minimum, where the quadratic model is unreliable. Using pure Newton on non-convex functions, where it can converge to maxima or saddles. Confusing Newton for roots (<span class=\"m\">f/f'</span>) with Newton for minimization (<span class=\"m\">f'/f''</span>).</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Newton's method fits a local quadratic using the Hessian and jumps to its minimum, solving <span class=\"m\">H\\mathbf d = -\\nabla f</span>. Near a minimum with positive definite Hessian it converges quadratically, and it solves quadratics in one step regardless of conditioning. Its costs (<span class=\"m\">n^2</span> memory, <span class=\"m\">n^3</span> time) and its behaviour on non-convex functions limit it to small or medium problems; quasi-Newton methods such as L-BFGS approximate it cheaply.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"newton\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Each step follows the tangent line down to the axis. Watch how quickly the digits settle.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. With <span class=\"m\">f(x) = x^2 - 2</span>, drag the start to <span class=\"m\">x_0 = 1</span> and press Step four times. Compare the iterates with <span class=\"m\">1.5</span>, <span class=\"m\">1.41667</span>, <span class=\"m\">1.4142157</span> from the article.\n2. Start far away, at <span class=\"m\">x_0 = 3</span>, and count how many steps it takes to get 8 correct digits.\n3. Choose <span class=\"m\">\\cos x - x</span> and step from a few different starts.\n4. Choose <span class=\"m\">x^3 - 2x + 2</span> and start at <span class=\"m\">x_0 = 0</span>. The iterates jump to 1 and back to 0 forever.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> once close, the number of correct digits roughly doubles each step, the quadratic convergence of the article. The last function shows the other side: from a bad start Newton's method can cycle or wander, which is why practical versions add safeguards such as line searches.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{roots: }x_{n+1} = x_n - \\frac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}\\qquad\\text{minimization: }x_{n+1} = x_n - \\frac{f'(x_n)}{f''(x_n)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\nabla^2f(\\theta)\\,\\mathbf d = -\\nabla f(\\theta),\\qquad\\theta\\leftarrow\\theta + \\mathbf d</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">|x_{n+1} - x^*|\\le C|x_n - x^*|^2\\qquad\\text{(quadratic convergence)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{secant: }B_{t+1}\\mathbf s_t = \\mathbf y_t,\\ \\mathbf s_t = \\theta_{t+1} - \\theta_t,\\ \\mathbf y_t = \\nabla f_{t+1} - \\nabla f_t\\qquad\\text{Gauss-Newton: }H\\approx J^TJ</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Newton solves quadratics in one step</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">f(\\theta) = \\frac12\\theta^TA\\theta - \\mathbf b^T\\theta</span> with <span class=\"m\">A</span> positive definite, one Newton step from any <span class=\"m\">\\theta</span> lands on the minimizer <span class=\"m\">A^{-1}\\mathbf b</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-title\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\nabla f = A\\theta - \\mathbf b</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\nabla^2f = A</span>. The Newton step gives <span class=\"m\">\\theta - A^{-1}(A\\theta - \\mathbf b) = A^{-1}\\mathbf b</span>, where the gradient is zero.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Local quadratic convergence</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">f'(x^*) = 0</span>, <span class=\"m\">f''(x^*) &gt; 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">f''</span> is Lipschitz near <span class=\"m\">x^*</span>, then Newton's method started close enough to <span class=\"m\">x^*</span> converges quadratically.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-title\">Sketch</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Let <span class=\"m\">e_n = x_n - x^*</span>. Taylor expansion gives <span class=\"m\">0 = f'(x^*) = f'(x_n) - f''(x_n)e_n + O(e_n^2)</span>. Dividing by <span class=\"m\">f''(x_n)</span>, <span class=\"m\">e_n - \\frac{f'(x_n)}{f''(x_n)} = O(e_n^2)</span>, and the left side is <span class=\"m\">e_{n+1}</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Newton's method for <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt2</span> from <span class=\"m\">x_0 = 1</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">f(x) = x^2 - 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">f'(x) = 2x</span>: <span class=\"m\">x_1 = 1 - \\frac{-1}2 = 1.5</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">x_2 = 1.5 - \\frac{0.25}3 = 1.41667</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">x_3 = 1.41667 - \\frac{0.0069444}{2.83333}\\approx1.4142157</span>, already correct to 5 decimal places.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Minimize <span class=\"m\">f(x) = x - \\ln x</span> from <span class=\"m\">x_0 = 0.5</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">f'(x) = 1 - 1/x</span> and <span class=\"m\">f''(x) = 1/x^2</span>, so <span class=\"m\">x - f'/f'' = x - (x^2 - x) = 2x - x^2</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">x_1 = 0.75</span>, <span class=\"m\">x_2 = 0.9375</span>, <span class=\"m\">x_3 = 0.99609</span>.</li>\n<li>The error squares exactly: <span class=\"m\">1 - x_{n+1} = (1 - x_n)^2</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">One Newton step for <span class=\"m\">f(x, y) = x^2 + xy + y^2 - 3x</span> from <span class=\"m\">(0, 0)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Gradient <span class=\"m\">(2x + y - 3,\\ x + 2y) = (-3, 0)</span>; Hessian <span class=\"m\">H = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span>.</li>\n<li>Solve <span class=\"m\">H\\mathbf d = (3, 0)</span>: <span class=\"m\">H^{-1} = \\frac13\\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; -1\\\\-1 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf d = (2, -1)</span>.</li>\n<li>New point <span class=\"m\">(2, -1)</span>: gradient <span class=\"m\">(4 - 1 - 3,\\ 2 - 2) = (0, 0)</span>. The minimum in one step, since <span class=\"m\">f</span> is quadratic.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Let <span class=\"m\">f(x) = e^x - 2x</span>. (a) Find the minimizer exactly. (b) Write the Newton update and run three steps from <span class=\"m\">x_0 = 0</span>. (c) Run one step of gradient descent from <span class=\"m\">x_0 = 0</span> with <span class=\"m\">\\eta = 0.5</span> and compare. (d) Why is Newton's method not used directly for a network with <span class=\"m\">10^6</span> parameters?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) <span class=\"m\">f'(x) = e^x - 2 = 0</span> at <span class=\"m\">x = \\ln2\\approx0.6931</span>, and <span class=\"m\">f'' = e^x &gt; 0</span>, so it is the minimum.\n(b) <span class=\"m\">x\\leftarrow x - \\frac{e^x - 2}{e^x} = x - 1 + 2e^{-x}</span>. <span class=\"m\">x_1 = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">x_2 = 2/e\\approx0.7358</span>, <span class=\"m\">x_3\\approx-0.2642 + 2(0.4791)\\approx0.6940</span>.\n(c) <span class=\"m\">x_1 = 0 - 0.5(1 - 2) = 0.5</span>, still <span class=\"m\">0.19</span> away, while Newton is <span class=\"m\">0.0009</span> away after three steps.\n(d) The Hessian would have <span class=\"m\">10^{12}</span> entries (about 8 TB) and solving with it costs about <span class=\"m\">10^{18}</span> operations; quasi-Newton or first-order methods are used instead.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>L-BFGS is the default solver for logistic regression in scikit-learn and is widely used for small and medium smooth problems. Deep learning uses cheaper curvature ideas: Hessian-vector products computed by autodiff, diagonal approximations, and structured approximations such as K-FAC and Shampoo. Adam's division by <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{\\hat{\\mathbf v}}</span> can be seen as a crude diagonal curvature correction.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Processors and math libraries compute square roots and reciprocals with Newton iterations: <span class=\"m\">x\\leftarrow x(2 - ax)</span> converges quadratically to <span class=\"m\">1/a</span> using only multiplications. The famous \"fast inverse square root\" trick in early 3D games used one Newton step after a clever initial guess.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A GPS receiver computes its position by solving nonlinear equations relating its unknown location and clock error to the measured distances from several satellites. It does so with a few Gauss-Newton iterations, which converge so fast that the position updates many times per second.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Newton's method for <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt2</span> (root of <span class=\"m\">x^2 - 2</span>) from <span class=\"m\">x = 1</span>. What is the next iterate?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1.5|3/2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">1 - \\frac{1 - 2}{2} = 1.5</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Newton's method minimizes <span class=\"m\">f(x) = x^2 - 4x + 1</span> starting from <span class=\"m\">x = 10</span>. Where is it after one step?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>A quadratic is minimized in one Newton step: <span class=\"m\">10 - \\frac{16}2 = 2</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Newton's minimization step is equivalent to gradient descent with learning rate...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">1/f''(x)</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">f''(x)</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">f'(x)</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">x - f'(x)/f''(x)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What can go wrong if the Hessian has a negative eigenvalue?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">The Newton step may move toward a maximum or saddle.</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Nothing; Newton always decreases <span class=\"m\">f</span>.</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">The gradient becomes zero.</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The quadratic model has no minimum along that direction.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">If the error is <span class=\"m\">10^{-3}</span> and convergence is quadratic with <span class=\"m\">C = 1</span>, what is the error after one more step?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0.000001|1/1000000\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">(10^{-3})^2 = 10^{-6}</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What does L-BFGS store instead of the Hessian?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">The last few step and gradient-change pairs</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">The full inverse Hessian</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Only the current gradient</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>A handful of <span class=\"m\">(\\mathbf s, \\mathbf y)</span> pairs gives memory linear in <span class=\"m\">n</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "kkt": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"optimizing-with-rules-attached\" data-h=\"3\">Optimizing with rules attached</h3>\n<p>Most real decisions come with limits. You want the best laptop you can buy with 800 euros, the strongest bridge you can build with a fixed amount of steel, or the most accurate classifier whose weights are not too large. In each case the best unconstrained answer is usually not allowed, and the real optimum sits on the boundary of what is permitted. This lesson develops the standard tools for such problems: Lagrange multipliers for equality constraints, the KKT conditions for inequality constraints, and duality, which looks at the same problem from the point of view of prices.</p>\n<h3 id=\"equality-constraints-and-parallel-gradients\" data-h=\"3\">Equality constraints and parallel gradients</h3>\n<p>Imagine walking along a circular path on a hillside, trying to reach the highest point of the path, not of the hill. While you walk, the height changes unless your direction of travel is level. At the highest point on the path, moving along the path does not change your height to first order: the uphill direction (the gradient of the height) must be perpendicular to the path. The gradient of the constraint function is also perpendicular to the path. So at the optimum, the two gradients are parallel.</p>\n<p>Formally, to optimize <span class=\"m\">f(\\mathbf x)</span> subject to <span class=\"m\">h(\\mathbf x) = 0</span>, we look for points where <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f(\\mathbf x) + \\mu\\nabla h(\\mathbf x) = \\mathbf 0</span> for some number <span class=\"m\">\\mu</span>, together with <span class=\"m\">h(\\mathbf x) = 0</span>. Packaging this with the <strong>Lagrangian</strong>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\mathcal L(\\mathbf x, \\mu) = f(\\mathbf x) + \\mu\\,h(\\mathbf x)</span>\nthe conditions are simply that all partial derivatives of <span class=\"m\">\\mathcal L</span> are zero. The number <span class=\"m\">\\mu</span> is the <strong>Lagrange multiplier</strong>.</p>\n<p>Try it: minimize <span class=\"m\">x^2 + y^2</span> subject to <span class=\"m\">x + y = 2</span>. The Lagrangian is <span class=\"m\">x^2 + y^2 + \\mu(x + y - 2)</span>. Setting derivatives to zero gives <span class=\"m\">2x + \\mu = 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">2y + \\mu = 0</span>, so <span class=\"m\">x = y</span>, and the constraint forces <span class=\"m\">x = y = 1</span> with <span class=\"m\">\\mu = -2</span>. The minimum value is 2: the closest point to the origin on the line.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Lagrangian</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For <span class=\"m\">\\min f(\\mathbf x)</span> subject to <span class=\"m\">g_i(\\mathbf x)\\le0</span> and <span class=\"m\">h_j(\\mathbf x) = 0</span>, the function <span class=\"m\">\\mathcal L(\\mathbf x, \\boldsymbol\\lambda, \\boldsymbol\\mu) = f(\\mathbf x) + \\sum_i\\lambda_ig_i(\\mathbf x) + \\sum_j\\mu_jh_j(\\mathbf x)</span>. The numbers <span class=\"m\">\\lambda_i</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\mu_j</span> are Lagrange multipliers.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"multipliers-are-prices\" data-h=\"3\">Multipliers are prices</h3>\n<p>The multiplier has a meaning. If we relax the constraint to <span class=\"m\">x + y = c</span>, the optimum is <span class=\"m\">x = y = c/2</span> with value <span class=\"m\">f^*(c) = c^2/2</span>, whose derivative at <span class=\"m\">c = 2</span> is 2. That is <span class=\"m\">-\\mu</span>. In general, the multiplier tells you how much the optimal value would change per unit of relaxation of the constraint. Economists call it a <strong>shadow price</strong>: how much you should be willing to pay for one more unit of a limited resource.</p>\n<h3 id=\"inequality-constraints\" data-h=\"3\">Inequality constraints</h3>\n<p>Now the constraint is <span class=\"m\">g(\\mathbf x)\\le0</span>. Two things can happen at the optimum. Either the constraint is <strong>inactive</strong> (<span class=\"m\">g &lt; 0</span>): the optimum is inside the allowed region, the constraint does not matter, and its multiplier is <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = 0</span>. Or it is <strong>active</strong> (<span class=\"m\">g = 0</span>): the optimum is pressed against the boundary, and the gradient of <span class=\"m\">f</span> must point \"outward\", so that moving inward would increase <span class=\"m\">f</span>. This is expressed by <span class=\"m\">\\lambda\\ge0</span> in the condition <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f + \\lambda\\nabla g = \\mathbf 0</span>. Both cases are summarized by <strong>complementary slackness</strong>: <span class=\"m\">\\lambda\\,g(\\mathbf x) = 0</span>.</p>\n<p>Example: minimize <span class=\"m\">(x - 3)^2</span> subject to <span class=\"m\">x\\le1</span>, that is <span class=\"m\">g = x - 1\\le0</span>. The unconstrained minimum <span class=\"m\">x = 3</span> is not allowed, so the constraint is active and <span class=\"m\">x = 1</span>. Stationarity <span class=\"m\">2(x - 3) + \\lambda = 0</span> gives <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = 4\\ge0</span>, as required. If instead the constraint were <span class=\"m\">x\\le5</span>, the unconstrained minimum <span class=\"m\">x = 3</span> would be allowed, the constraint would be inactive, and <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = 0</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-kkt-conditions\" data-h=\"3\">The KKT conditions</h3>\n<p>For <span class=\"m\">\\min f</span> subject to <span class=\"m\">g_i\\le0</span> and <span class=\"m\">h_j = 0</span>, the <strong>Karush-Kuhn-Tucker (KKT) conditions</strong> are: stationarity, <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f + \\sum_i\\lambda_i\\nabla g_i + \\sum_j\\mu_j\\nabla h_j = \\mathbf 0</span>; primal feasibility, <span class=\"m\">g_i\\le0</span> and <span class=\"m\">h_j = 0</span>; dual feasibility, <span class=\"m\">\\lambda_i\\ge0</span>; and complementary slackness, <span class=\"m\">\\lambda_ig_i = 0</span> for every <span class=\"m\">i</span>. Under mild regularity assumptions they are necessary at any optimum, and for convex problems they are also sufficient. To solve small problems by hand, guess which constraints are active, solve the resulting equations, and check that all conditions hold.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Complementary slackness</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The KKT condition <span class=\"m\">\\lambda_ig_i(\\mathbf x^*) = 0</span>: for each inequality constraint, either the constraint is tight or its multiplier is zero.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"duality\" data-h=\"3\">Duality</h3>\n<p>Duality turns a constrained minimization into a maximization over multipliers. The <strong>dual function</strong> is <span class=\"m\">q(\\boldsymbol\\lambda, \\boldsymbol\\mu) = \\min_{\\mathbf x}\\mathcal L(\\mathbf x, \\boldsymbol\\lambda, \\boldsymbol\\mu)</span>. For any <span class=\"m\">\\boldsymbol\\lambda\\ge\\mathbf 0</span>, <span class=\"m\">q</span> is a lower bound on the optimal value <span class=\"m\">f^*</span>; this is <strong>weak duality</strong>. The <strong>dual problem</strong> finds the best such bound, <span class=\"m\">\\max_{\\boldsymbol\\lambda\\ge0,\\boldsymbol\\mu}q</span>. For convex problems satisfying a mild condition (Slater's condition: some strictly feasible point exists), the bound is tight, <span class=\"m\">\\max q = f^*</span>, which is <strong>strong duality</strong>.</p>\n<p>For example, minimize <span class=\"m\">x^2</span> subject to <span class=\"m\">x\\ge1</span>, written as <span class=\"m\">g = 1 - x\\le0</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">\\mathcal L = x^2 + \\lambda(1 - x)</span>, minimized at <span class=\"m\">x = \\lambda/2</span>, giving <span class=\"m\">q(\\lambda) = \\lambda - \\lambda^2/4</span>. This is maximized at <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = 2</span> with <span class=\"m\">q = 1</span>, which equals the primal optimum <span class=\"m\">f(1) = 1</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Allowing negative multipliers on inequality constraints written as <span class=\"m\">g\\le0</span>. Forgetting to check the inactive case, or the active case, when solving by hand. Mixing sign conventions: some texts write <span class=\"m\">f - \\lambda g</span>, which flips the signs of the multipliers. Applying KKT as sufficient for non-convex problems, where a KKT point can be a saddle or a local optimum only.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>At a constrained optimum, the gradient of the objective is balanced by a combination of constraint gradients, which the Lagrangian expresses compactly. Multipliers are shadow prices. Inequality constraints add <span class=\"m\">\\lambda\\ge0</span> and complementary slackness. The KKT conditions collect all of this, and duality gives lower bounds that are tight for well-behaved convex problems.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"lagrange\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Maximize <span class=\"m\">f = x + 2y</span> on the unit circle. Slide along the constraint and watch the two gradients.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Move the slider slowly around the circle and watch the value of <span class=\"m\">f</span>.\n2. Find the position where the arrows for <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\nabla g</span> line up. The readout reports the optimum, <span class=\"m\">f = \\sqrt5\\approx2.236</span>, at <span class=\"m\">(1/\\sqrt5, 2/\\sqrt5)\\approx(0.447, 0.894)</span>.\n3. Continue to the opposite side, where the gradients are parallel but point in opposite directions. That is the minimum, <span class=\"m\">-\\sqrt5</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> at both extremes the gradient of <span class=\"m\">f</span> is perpendicular to the circle, exactly the parallel-gradient condition <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f = \\lambda\\nabla g</span> from the article. Everywhere else <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f</span> has a component along the circle, so moving along the path would improve <span class=\"m\">f</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\mathcal L(\\mathbf x, \\boldsymbol\\lambda, \\boldsymbol\\mu) = f(\\mathbf x) + \\sum_i\\lambda_ig_i(\\mathbf x) + \\sum_j\\mu_jh_j(\\mathbf x)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{KKT: }\\nabla f + \\sum_i\\lambda_i\\nabla g_i + \\sum_j\\mu_j\\nabla h_j = \\mathbf 0,\\quad g_i\\le0,\\ h_j = 0,\\quad\\lambda_i\\ge0,\\quad\\lambda_ig_i = 0</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">q(\\boldsymbol\\lambda, \\boldsymbol\\mu) = \\min_{\\mathbf x}\\mathcal L\\le f^*\\ \\ (\\boldsymbol\\lambda\\ge0)\\qquad\\text{strong duality: }\\max q = f^*</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\frac{df^*}{dc} = -\\mu\\ \\text{ for the constraint }h(\\mathbf x) - c = 0</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">KKT conditions</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x^*</span> is a local minimum and a constraint qualification holds (for example, the gradients of the active constraints are linearly independent), then there exist multipliers satisfying the KKT conditions. If the problem is convex (<span class=\"m\">f</span> and <span class=\"m\">g_i</span> convex, <span class=\"m\">h_j</span> affine), any point satisfying the KKT conditions is a global minimum.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Weak duality</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For any <span class=\"m\">\\boldsymbol\\lambda\\ge\\mathbf 0</span> and any <span class=\"m\">\\boldsymbol\\mu</span>, <span class=\"m\">q(\\boldsymbol\\lambda, \\boldsymbol\\mu)\\le f(\\mathbf x)</span> for every feasible <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-title\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For feasible <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x</span>, <span class=\"m\">g_i(\\mathbf x)\\le0</span> and <span class=\"m\">h_j(\\mathbf x) = 0</span>, so with <span class=\"m\">\\lambda_i\\ge0</span> the extra terms of <span class=\"m\">\\mathcal L</span> are <span class=\"m\">\\le0</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\mathcal L(\\mathbf x, \\boldsymbol\\lambda, \\boldsymbol\\mu)\\le f(\\mathbf x)</span>. The minimum over all <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x</span> is even smaller: <span class=\"m\">q\\le\\mathcal L(\\mathbf x, \\boldsymbol\\lambda, \\boldsymbol\\mu)\\le f(\\mathbf x)</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Maximize <span class=\"m\">x + 2y</span> subject to <span class=\"m\">x^2 + y^2 = 1</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Stationarity: <span class=\"m\">(1, 2) = \\lambda(2x, 2y)</span>, so <span class=\"m\">x = \\frac1{2\\lambda}</span> and <span class=\"m\">y = \\frac1\\lambda</span>.</li>\n<li>Constraint: <span class=\"m\">\\frac1{4\\lambda^2} + \\frac1{\\lambda^2} = 1</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\lambda^2 = \\frac54</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = \\frac{\\sqrt5}2</span> for the maximum.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">x = \\frac1{\\sqrt5}</span>, <span class=\"m\">y = \\frac2{\\sqrt5}</span>, and <span class=\"m\">f = \\frac5{\\sqrt5} = \\sqrt5\\approx2.236</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Minimize <span class=\"m\">(x - 3)^2</span> subject to <span class=\"m\">x\\le1</span>, then subject to <span class=\"m\">x\\le5</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>With <span class=\"m\">x\\le1</span>: try inactive (<span class=\"m\">\\lambda = 0</span>), giving <span class=\"m\">x = 3</span>, which is infeasible. Try active: <span class=\"m\">x = 1</span>, and <span class=\"m\">2(1 - 3) + \\lambda = 0</span> gives <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = 4\\ge0</span>. Optimum <span class=\"m\">x = 1</span>, value 4.</li>\n<li>With <span class=\"m\">x\\le5</span>: inactive, <span class=\"m\">x = 3</span> is feasible and <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = 0</span>. Optimum value 0.</li>\n<li>The multiplier 4 in the first case is the shadow price: relaxing to <span class=\"m\">x\\le1 + \\epsilon</span> lowers the value by about <span class=\"m\">4\\epsilon</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">The dual of <span class=\"m\">\\min x^2</span> subject to <span class=\"m\">x\\ge1</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\mathcal L = x^2 + \\lambda(1 - x)</span>; minimizing over <span class=\"m\">x</span> gives <span class=\"m\">x = \\lambda/2</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">q(\\lambda) = \\frac{\\lambda^2}4 + \\lambda - \\frac{\\lambda^2}2 = \\lambda - \\frac{\\lambda^2}4</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">q'(\\lambda) = 1 - \\frac\\lambda2 = 0</span> at <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = 2</span>, so <span class=\"m\">q = 1</span>, equal to the primal optimum <span class=\"m\">f(1) = 1</span>: strong duality.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Maximize <span class=\"m\">xy</span> subject to <span class=\"m\">x + y\\le4</span>, <span class=\"m\">x\\ge0</span>, <span class=\"m\">y\\ge0</span>. (a) Write it as a minimization in standard form with its Lagrangian. (b) Solve the KKT conditions. (c) Interpret the multiplier of <span class=\"m\">x + y\\le4</span> and verify it by relaxing the constraint.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) Minimize <span class=\"m\">-xy</span> subject to <span class=\"m\">x + y - 4\\le0</span>, <span class=\"m\">-x\\le0</span>, <span class=\"m\">-y\\le0</span>; <span class=\"m\">\\mathcal L = -xy + \\lambda(x + y - 4) - \\nu_1x - \\nu_2y</span>.\n(b) At the optimum <span class=\"m\">x, y &gt; 0</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\nu_1 = \\nu_2 = 0</span>. Stationarity: <span class=\"m\">-y + \\lambda = 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">-x + \\lambda = 0</span>, so <span class=\"m\">x = y = \\lambda</span>. If <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = 0</span>, then <span class=\"m\">x = y = 0</span>, which is not optimal. So the constraint is active: <span class=\"m\">2\\lambda = 4</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">x = y = 2</span>, and the maximum is <span class=\"m\">xy = 4</span>.\n(c) <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = 2</span> is the shadow price of the budget. With <span class=\"m\">x + y\\le c</span>, the optimum is <span class=\"m\">c^2/4</span>, whose derivative at <span class=\"m\">c = 4</span> is 2.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Support vector machines are solved through their dual. Complementary slackness means only the training points on or inside the margin, the support vectors, have nonzero multipliers, and the dual depends on the data only through inner products, which enables the kernel trick. Lagrangian methods are also used for fairness constraints and constrained reinforcement learning.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The max-flow min-cut theorem is an instance of strong duality: the largest flow through a network equals the capacity of its smallest cut. Duality is also the basis of many approximation algorithms, where a dual solution certifies how close a found solution is to optimal.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>In consumer economics, a shopper maximizes satisfaction subject to a budget. The Lagrange multiplier on the budget constraint is the marginal utility of money: how much extra satisfaction one more euro would buy. Electricity markets publish such shadow prices, the cost of supplying one more megawatt-hour at each location, as their actual prices.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">If an inequality constraint is not tight at the optimum, its KKT multiplier is...</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Complementary slackness: <span class=\"m\">\\lambda_ig_i = 0</span> with <span class=\"m\">g_i &lt; 0</span> forces <span class=\"m\">\\lambda_i = 0</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For constraints written as <span class=\"m\">g_i(\\mathbf x)\\le0</span> in a minimization, the multipliers must be...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">nonnegative</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">nonpositive</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">zero</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Dual feasibility: <span class=\"m\">\\lambda_i\\ge0</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the minimum of <span class=\"m\">x^2 + y^2</span> subject to <span class=\"m\">x + y = 4</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"8\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>By symmetry <span class=\"m\">x = y = 2</span>, giving <span class=\"m\">4 + 4</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For <span class=\"m\">\\min(x - 3)^2</span> subject to <span class=\"m\">x\\le1</span>, what is the multiplier <span class=\"m\">\\lambda</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"4\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">2(1 - 3) + \\lambda = 0</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Weak duality says that for any <span class=\"m\">\\boldsymbol\\lambda\\ge0</span> the dual function is...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">a lower bound on the primal optimum</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">an upper bound on the primal optimum</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">equal to the primal optimum</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Equality (strong duality) needs extra conditions such as convexity and Slater's condition.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">At a constrained optimum with one equality constraint, the gradients of <span class=\"m\">f</span> and <span class=\"m\">h</span> are...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">parallel</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">perpendicular</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">both zero</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\nabla f = -\\mu\\nabla h</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "linear-programming": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"a-workshop-s-production-plan\" data-h=\"3\">A workshop's production plan</h3>\n<p>A small workshop makes tables and chairs. Each table earns 3 hundred euros of profit and each chair 2 hundred. Making <span class=\"m\">x</span> tables and <span class=\"m\">y</span> chairs uses <span class=\"m\">x + y</span> units of wood, of which 6 are available per week; <span class=\"m\">x + 3y</span> hours of finishing, of which there are 12; and <span class=\"m\">2x + y</span> hours of cutting, of which there are 10. How many of each should it make? This is a <strong>linear program</strong> (LP): maximize <span class=\"m\">3x + 2y</span> subject to <span class=\"m\">x + y\\le6</span>, <span class=\"m\">x + 3y\\le12</span>, <span class=\"m\">2x + y\\le10</span>, <span class=\"m\">x\\ge0</span>, <span class=\"m\">y\\ge0</span>. The objective and every constraint are linear, which makes the problem both very structured and very common: airlines, factories, refineries and delivery companies solve LPs with millions of variables every day.</p>\n<h3 id=\"standard-form-and-the-feasible-region\" data-h=\"3\">Standard form and the feasible region</h3>\n<p>In general an LP is\n<span class=\"mb\">\\max\\ \\mathbf c^T\\mathbf x\\quad\\text{subject to}\\quad A\\mathbf x\\le\\mathbf b,\\ \\mathbf x\\ge\\mathbf 0</span>\nEach inequality cuts the plane (or space) in half along a straight line (or flat plane), and the feasible region is the intersection of these half-spaces: a convex polygon in two dimensions, a <strong>polytope</strong> in general. For the workshop, the region has five corners: <span class=\"m\">(0, 0)</span>, <span class=\"m\">(5, 0)</span>, <span class=\"m\">(4, 2)</span>, <span class=\"m\">(3, 3)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(0, 4)</span>. For instance <span class=\"m\">(4, 2)</span> is where the wood line <span class=\"m\">x + y = 6</span> meets the cutting line <span class=\"m\">2x + y = 10</span>; subtracting the equations gives <span class=\"m\">x = 4</span>, then <span class=\"m\">y = 2</span>. It satisfies the finishing constraint too: <span class=\"m\">4 + 6 = 10\\le12</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Linear program</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>An optimization problem with a linear objective and linear equality or inequality constraints, such as <span class=\"m\">\\max\\mathbf c^T\\mathbf x</span> subject to <span class=\"m\">A\\mathbf x\\le\\mathbf b</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x\\ge\\mathbf 0</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Vertex (corner point)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A point of the feasible polytope that is not between two other feasible points; in <span class=\"m\">n</span> dimensions it is where <span class=\"m\">n</span> linearly independent constraints are tight.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"the-corner-point-principle\" data-h=\"3\">The corner-point principle</h3>\n<p>Draw the lines where the profit <span class=\"m\">3x + 2y</span> equals a constant: they are parallel. Increasing the constant slides the line across the region. The largest constant for which the line still touches the region is the optimum, and the last point touched is always a corner (or a whole edge, if the line is parallel to it). So we only need to check corners: <span class=\"m\">(0, 0)</span> gives 0, <span class=\"m\">(5, 0)</span> gives 15, <span class=\"m\">(4, 2)</span> gives 16, <span class=\"m\">(3, 3)</span> gives 15 and <span class=\"m\">(0, 4)</span> gives 8. The best plan is 4 tables and 2 chairs, for a profit of 16 hundred euros. This <strong>corner-point principle</strong> is the central fact of linear programming.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-simplex-method\" data-h=\"3\">The simplex method</h3>\n<p>Checking every corner is hopeless in high dimensions, where there can be astronomically many. The <strong>simplex method</strong>, invented by George Dantzig in 1947, walks from corner to neighbouring corner along edges, always improving the objective, and stops when no neighbour is better. For the workshop: start at <span class=\"m\">(0, 0)</span> with profit 0. Its neighbours are <span class=\"m\">(5, 0)</span> with 15 and <span class=\"m\">(0, 4)</span> with 8; move to <span class=\"m\">(5, 0)</span>. Its other neighbour, <span class=\"m\">(4, 2)</span>, gives 16; move there. The neighbours of <span class=\"m\">(4, 2)</span> are <span class=\"m\">(5, 0)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(3, 3)</span>, both worth 15. No improvement is possible, and because the region is convex and the objective linear, a corner with no better neighbour is a global optimum.</p>\n<p>In the worst case the simplex method can visit exponentially many corners, but in practice it needs a number of steps roughly proportional to the number of constraints. <strong>Interior-point methods</strong> take a different route through the inside of the region and are guaranteed to run in polynomial time.</p>\n<h3 id=\"when-things-go-wrong\" data-h=\"3\">When things go wrong</h3>\n<p>Three special cases appear. An LP is <strong>infeasible</strong> if the constraints contradict each other, such as <span class=\"m\">x\\le1</span> and <span class=\"m\">x\\ge2</span>. It is <strong>unbounded</strong> if the objective can grow forever, such as maximizing <span class=\"m\">x</span> subject only to <span class=\"m\">x\\ge0</span>. And it can have <strong>multiple optima</strong> when the objective is parallel to an edge: maximizing <span class=\"m\">x + y</span> for the workshop gives 6 at both <span class=\"m\">(4, 2)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(3, 3)</span>, and at every point on the edge between them.</p>\n<h3 id=\"duality-and-shadow-prices\" data-h=\"3\">Duality and shadow prices</h3>\n<p>Every LP has a <strong>dual</strong>. For <span class=\"m\">\\max\\mathbf c^T\\mathbf x</span> subject to <span class=\"m\">A\\mathbf x\\le\\mathbf b</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x\\ge\\mathbf 0</span>, the dual is <span class=\"m\">\\min\\mathbf b^T\\mathbf y</span> subject to <span class=\"m\">A^T\\mathbf y\\ge\\mathbf c</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf y\\ge\\mathbf 0</span>. The dual variables are prices for the resources. For the workshop, the finishing constraint is not tight at the optimum, so its price is 0. The prices <span class=\"m\">y_1</span> (wood) and <span class=\"m\">y_3</span> (cutting) satisfy <span class=\"m\">y_1 + 2y_3 = 3</span> and <span class=\"m\">y_1 + y_3 = 2</span>, giving <span class=\"m\">y_1 = y_3 = 1</span>. The dual objective is <span class=\"m\">6\\cdot1 + 12\\cdot0 + 10\\cdot1 = 16</span>, exactly the best profit. That equality is LP strong duality. One more unit of wood would raise the profit by about 1 hundred euros; more finishing time would be worthless.</p>\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Checking only some corners, or including intersection points of constraint lines that lie outside the feasible region. Forgetting the nonnegativity constraints. Assuming an optimum exists without checking for unboundedness. Rounding an LP solution to integers and assuming it is the best integer solution: integer programming is a different and much harder problem.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>A linear program optimizes a linear objective over a convex polytope defined by linear inequalities. The optimum, if it exists, is at a corner. The simplex method walks between neighbouring corners until no neighbour is better. LPs can be infeasible, unbounded or have many optima. The dual LP assigns prices to resources, and its optimal value equals the primal optimum.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g05-lp\" data-args='{&quot;c1&quot;: 3, &quot;c2&quot;: 2}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> The workshop LP. The shaded polygon is the feasible region, the lines are profit contours, and the corners are labelled with their profit.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. With <span class=\"m\">c_1 = 3</span> and <span class=\"m\">c_2 = 2</span>, read the corner values and confirm the best corner is <span class=\"m\">(4, 2)</span> with value 16.\n2. Press Reset walk, then Simplex step repeatedly. The walk goes <span class=\"m\">(0, 0)\\to(5, 0)\\to(4, 2)</span> and stops.\n3. Set <span class=\"m\">c_1 = c_2 = 1</span>. Two corners tie at 6: the contour line is parallel to an edge, so there are many optima.\n4. Make <span class=\"m\">c_1</span> negative and see the best corner move to the <span class=\"m\">y</span>-axis. Drag the test point outside the region to see a constraint fail.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> changing the objective only tilts the contour lines; the optimum jumps from corner to corner and never lands inside the polygon. That is the corner-point principle from the article, and the simplex walk is a local search that is guaranteed to be global because the region is convex.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\max\\ \\mathbf c^T\\mathbf x\\ \\ \\text{s.t.}\\ A\\mathbf x\\le\\mathbf b,\\ \\mathbf x\\ge\\mathbf 0\\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad\\min\\ \\mathbf b^T\\mathbf y\\ \\ \\text{s.t.}\\ A^T\\mathbf y\\ge\\mathbf c,\\ \\mathbf y\\ge\\mathbf 0</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{weak duality: }\\mathbf c^T\\mathbf x\\le\\mathbf b^T\\mathbf y\\qquad\\text{strong duality: }\\max\\mathbf c^T\\mathbf x = \\min\\mathbf b^T\\mathbf y</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{complementary slackness: }y_i\\,(b_i - (A\\mathbf x)_i) = 0</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Corner-point principle</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If a linear program has a bounded feasible polytope that is nonempty, its optimum is attained at a vertex.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-title\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Every point of a bounded polytope is a convex combination of its vertices: <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x = \\sum_k\\alpha_k\\mathbf v_k</span> with <span class=\"m\">\\alpha_k\\ge0</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\sum_k\\alpha_k = 1</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf c^T\\mathbf x = \\sum_k\\alpha_k\\mathbf c^T\\mathbf v_k\\le\\max_k\\mathbf c^T\\mathbf v_k</span>. So no point beats the best vertex.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Weak duality for LP</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x</span> is feasible for the primal and <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf y</span> for the dual, then <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf c^T\\mathbf x\\le\\mathbf b^T\\mathbf y</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-title\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Since <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x\\ge\\mathbf 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">A^T\\mathbf y\\ge\\mathbf c</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf c^T\\mathbf x\\le(A^T\\mathbf y)^T\\mathbf x = \\mathbf y^TA\\mathbf x</span>. Since <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf y\\ge\\mathbf 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">A\\mathbf x\\le\\mathbf b</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf y^TA\\mathbf x\\le\\mathbf y^T\\mathbf b</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Find the corners of the workshop region</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Axes: <span class=\"m\">(0, 0)</span>; on <span class=\"m\">y = 0</span> the tightest bound is <span class=\"m\">2x\\le10</span>, giving <span class=\"m\">(5, 0)</span>; on <span class=\"m\">x = 0</span> it is <span class=\"m\">3y\\le12</span>, giving <span class=\"m\">(0, 4)</span>.</li>\n<li>Wood meets cutting: <span class=\"m\">x + y = 6</span> and <span class=\"m\">2x + y = 10</span> give <span class=\"m\">(4, 2)</span>.</li>\n<li>Wood meets finishing: <span class=\"m\">x + y = 6</span> and <span class=\"m\">x + 3y = 12</span> give <span class=\"m\">2y = 6</span>, so <span class=\"m\">(3, 3)</span>; check <span class=\"m\">2\\cdot3 + 3 = 9\\le10</span>.</li>\n<li>Finishing meets cutting at <span class=\"m\">x = 3.6</span>, <span class=\"m\">y = 2.8</span>, but <span class=\"m\">3.6 + 2.8 = 6.4 &gt; 6</span> violates the wood limit, so it is not a corner.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Compute the shadow prices for the workshop</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>At <span class=\"m\">(4, 2)</span> the tight constraints are wood and cutting; finishing has slack, so <span class=\"m\">y_2 = 0</span>.</li>\n<li>Dual constraints tight for <span class=\"m\">x, y &gt; 0</span>: <span class=\"m\">y_1 + 2y_3 = 3</span> and <span class=\"m\">y_1 + y_3 = 2</span>, so <span class=\"m\">y_3 = 1</span> and <span class=\"m\">y_1 = 1</span>.</li>\n<li>Dual value <span class=\"m\">6(1) + 12(0) + 10(1) = 16</span>, equal to the primal optimum.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A diet must provide at least 4 units of protein and 6 units of fibre. Food A costs 2 per unit and gives 1 protein and 1 fibre; food B costs 3 and gives 1 protein and 3 fibre. (a) Write the LP. (b) Solve it by the corner-point method. (c) Write the dual and verify that it has the same optimal value.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) Minimize <span class=\"m\">2x + 3y</span> subject to <span class=\"m\">x + y\\ge4</span>, <span class=\"m\">x + 3y\\ge6</span>, <span class=\"m\">x, y\\ge0</span>.\n(b) Corners of the (unbounded) feasible region: <span class=\"m\">(0, 4)</span> with cost 12, the intersection of <span class=\"m\">x + y = 4</span> and <span class=\"m\">x + 3y = 6</span> at <span class=\"m\">(3, 1)</span> with cost 9, and <span class=\"m\">(6, 0)</span> with cost 12. Since costs are positive, the minimum is at a corner: <span class=\"m\">(3, 1)</span>, cost 9.\n(c) Dual: maximize <span class=\"m\">4u + 6v</span> subject to <span class=\"m\">u + v\\le2</span>, <span class=\"m\">u + 3v\\le3</span>, <span class=\"m\">u, v\\ge0</span>. Both constraints are tight: <span class=\"m\">v = 0.5</span>, <span class=\"m\">u = 1.5</span>, value <span class=\"m\">6 + 3 = 9</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Least absolute deviations regression (<span class=\"m\">\\min\\sum_i|y_i - \\mathbf w^T\\mathbf x_i|</span>) and L1-regularized problems can be written as LPs. Optimal transport, which defines the Wasserstein distance between distributions, is an LP. LP relaxations are used for inference in graphical models and for verifying the robustness of neural networks.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Shortest paths, maximum flow, minimum-cost flow and bipartite matching can all be written as LPs. If variables must be integers, the problem becomes integer linear programming, which is NP-hard; solvers attack it with branch and bound using LP relaxations as bounds.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The diet problem was one of the first LPs ever solved, in the 1940s: find the cheapest combination of foods that meets every nutritional requirement. Today oil refineries use LPs to decide how to blend crude oils, and airlines use them to assign crews to flights at minimum cost.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Where does a bounded, feasible LP attain its optimum?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">At a vertex of the feasible region</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">At its centre</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Anywhere inside</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The corner-point principle.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the value of <span class=\"m\">3x + 2y</span> at the corner <span class=\"m\">(4, 2)</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"16\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">12 + 4 = 16</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For the workshop region, what is the maximum of <span class=\"m\">x + y</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"6\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>It is attained along the whole edge from <span class=\"m\">(4, 2)</span> to <span class=\"m\">(3, 3)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Maximize <span class=\"m\">x</span> subject only to <span class=\"m\">x\\ge0</span>. This LP is...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">unbounded</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">infeasible</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">optimal at <span class=\"m\">x = 0</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The objective can grow without limit.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A constraint has slack at the optimum. Its dual variable (shadow price) is...</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Complementary slackness: extra units of an unused resource are worth nothing.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What does the simplex method do at each step?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">Moves to a neighbouring vertex with a better objective value</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Moves to the centre of the region</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Checks every vertex</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>It stops at a vertex with no better neighbour, which is optimal.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "practical": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"theory-meets-messy-reality\" data-h=\"3\">Theory meets messy reality</h3>\n<p>The previous lessons gave clean guarantees for clean problems. Real training runs are messier: the loss is non-convex, the data is noisy, the code may contain bugs, and a single bad setting can waste days of computation. Experienced practitioners rely on a short list of habits that catch most problems early. This lesson collects them, and each one connects back to an idea you have already met.</p>\n<p>Imagine baking a new cake recipe that comes out flat. A good baker does not change five things at once. They check the oven temperature first, then whether the baking powder is fresh, and they bake a small test cake before committing to a wedding order. Debugging a training run works the same way: check the simplest things first, test small, and change one thing at a time.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sanity-checks-before-training\" data-h=\"3\">Sanity checks before training</h3>\n<p>Before training at all, compute the loss of the freshly initialized model. For a classifier with <span class=\"m\">K</span> classes whose initial predictions are nearly uniform, the cross-entropy should be close to <span class=\"m\">-\\ln(1/K) = \\ln K</span>. With 10 classes that is <span class=\"m\">\\ln10\\approx2.30</span>. If you see 50 instead, something is wrong with the labels, the loss function or the initialization. Next, try to <strong>overfit a single small batch</strong>: train on 10 to 100 examples until the training loss is nearly zero. A model that cannot memorize a handful of examples has a bug, such as a detached gradient, a wrong label alignment or a learning rate of zero. Finally, compare with a trivial baseline, such as always predicting the most common class, so you know what \"better than nothing\" looks like.</p>\n<h3 id=\"initialization\" data-h=\"3\">Initialization</h3>\n<p>Suppose a layer computes <span class=\"m\">z = \\sum_{i=1}^nw_ix_i</span> with independent zero-mean weights and inputs. Then <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Var}(z) = n\\operatorname{Var}(w)\\operatorname{Var}(x)</span>. If <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Var}(w)</span> is too large, signals grow layer after layer and explode; too small, and they shrink toward zero, along with the gradients from the backpropagation lesson. Choosing <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Var}(w) = 1/n</span> keeps the variance constant. ReLU zeroes half its inputs, which halves the variance, so <strong>He initialization</strong> uses <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Var}(w) = 2/n_{\\text{in}}</span>. <strong>Xavier (Glorot) initialization</strong> uses <span class=\"m\">2/(n_{\\text{in}} + n_{\\text{out}})</span>, balancing the forward and backward passes, and suits tanh. For a ReLU layer with 512 inputs, He initialization gives standard deviation <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{2/512} = 1/16 = 0.0625</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">He initialization</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Initializing weights with variance <span class=\"m\">2/n_{\\text{in}}</span>, where <span class=\"m\">n_{\\text{in}}</span> is the number of inputs to the layer, so that signal variance is preserved through ReLU layers.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"scale-your-inputs\" data-h=\"3\">Scale your inputs</h3>\n<p>Recall that gradient descent is slow when the condition number is large. Features measured on very different scales, such as age in years and income in euros, create exactly that kind of narrow valley. <strong>Standardizing</strong> each feature, <span class=\"m\">x\\mapsto(x - \\mu)/\\sigma</span> using the training set's mean and standard deviation, puts them on a common scale. A feature with mean 10 and standard deviation 2 maps the value 14 to <span class=\"m\">(14 - 10)/2 = 2</span>. Normalization layers inside networks (batch normalization, layer normalization) apply the same idea to hidden activations.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-learning-rate-comes-first\" data-h=\"3\">The learning rate comes first</h3>\n<p>If you tune only one hyperparameter, tune the learning rate. Sweep it on a logarithmic scale, for example <span class=\"m\">10^{-5}, 10^{-4}, \\ldots, 1</span>, train briefly with each, and pick the largest value that trains stably, or slightly below it. A <strong>learning-rate range test</strong> does this in a single run by increasing <span class=\"m\">\\eta</span> exponentially every few steps and plotting the loss; the best rate is usually a little below where the loss starts to rise sharply. Then add warmup and a decay schedule.</p>\n<h3 id=\"reading-the-loss-curve\" data-h=\"3\">Reading the loss curve</h3>\n<p>The training curve is your main diagnostic. A loss that becomes <strong>NaN</strong> or infinite usually means the learning rate is too high (the divergence we saw for <span class=\"m\">\\eta &gt; 2/L</span>), or there is a numerical problem such as <span class=\"m\">\\log0</span>. A loss that stays <strong>flat</strong> suggests a learning rate that is far too small, dead ReLUs, or labels that do not match the inputs. A loss that <strong>oscillates wildly</strong> suggests a learning rate a bit too high or a batch size too small. A <strong>training loss that keeps falling while the validation loss rises</strong> is overfitting: the model is memorizing the training set. Remedies are more data, data augmentation, regularization such as weight decay or dropout, and early stopping.</p>\n<h3 id=\"gradient-clipping\" data-h=\"3\">Gradient clipping</h3>\n<p>Occasionally a single batch produces a huge gradient, for example in recurrent networks or at the start of training, and one enormous step can wreck the model. <strong>Gradient clipping by norm</strong> rescales the gradient if its length exceeds a threshold <span class=\"m\">c</span>:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\mathbf g\\leftarrow\\mathbf g\\cdot\\min\\left(1, \\frac c{\\|\\mathbf g\\|}\\right)</span>\nWith <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf g = (3, 4)</span> and <span class=\"m\">c = 1</span>, the norm is 5, so the clipped gradient is <span class=\"m\">(0.6, 0.8)</span>: same direction, length 1. Clipping keeps the direction of descent while capping the step.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Gradient clipping</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Rescaling the gradient so that its norm does not exceed a threshold <span class=\"m\">c</span>, which prevents rare huge gradients from causing destructive updates.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"early-stopping-and-checkpoints\" data-h=\"3\">Early stopping and checkpoints</h3>\n<p>Evaluate on a held-out validation set regularly and save a <strong>checkpoint</strong> whenever the validation loss reaches a new best. <strong>Early stopping</strong> ends training when the validation loss has not improved for a set number of evaluations (the patience) and returns the best checkpoint. It is cheap, effective regularization, and it protects you from runs that crash late.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Early stopping</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Halting training when the validation loss stops improving and keeping the parameters from the best validation point, used to prevent overfitting.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Changing several hyperparameters at once, so you cannot tell what helped. Computing normalization statistics on the whole dataset, including validation and test data, which leaks information. Judging a run from a single noisy mini-batch loss. Tuning on the test set. Forgetting to switch the model to evaluation mode (for dropout and batch normalization) when measuring validation loss.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Check the initial loss against <span class=\"m\">\\ln K</span>, overfit one batch, and compare with a baseline. Initialize with He or Xavier variance so signals neither explode nor vanish, and standardize inputs to improve conditioning. Tune the learning rate first on a log scale, then read the loss curve to diagnose divergence, stalling or overfitting. Clip gradients by norm, and use validation checkpoints with early stopping.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"gd\" data-args='{&quot;surface&quot;: &quot;bowl&quot;, &quot;lr&quot;: 0.55}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> A learning rate just above the stability limit <span class=\"m\">2/L = 0.5</span>. This is what a diverging training run looks like.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Press Run with <span class=\"m\">\\eta = 0.55</span> and watch the loss readout grow until it reports divergence, the toy version of a NaN loss.\n2. Lower <span class=\"m\">\\eta</span> to <span class=\"m\">0.45</span> and run again: the path oscillates but converges, like a noisy but working training curve.\n3. Lower <span class=\"m\">\\eta</span> to <span class=\"m\">0.005</span> and run: the loss barely moves, the \"flat curve\" symptom.\n4. Find by trial the largest <span class=\"m\">\\eta</span> that still converges, as a learning-rate sweep would.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the three loss-curve symptoms in the article, divergence, oscillation and stalling, all come from the learning rate alone, which is why it is the first thing to tune and why lowering it is the first response to a NaN.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{initial cross-entropy}\\approx\\ln K\\qquad\\operatorname{Var}\\Big(\\sum_{i=1}^nw_ix_i\\Big) = n\\operatorname{Var}(w)\\operatorname{Var}(x)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{He: }\\operatorname{Var}(w) = \\frac2{n_{\\text{in}}}\\qquad\\text{Xavier: }\\operatorname{Var}(w) = \\frac2{n_{\\text{in}} + n_{\\text{out}}}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{standardize: }x\\mapsto\\frac{x - \\mu}\\sigma\\qquad\\text{clip: }\\mathbf g\\leftarrow\\mathbf g\\min\\Big(1, \\frac c{\\|\\mathbf g\\|}\\Big)</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Variance of a weighted sum</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">w_1, \\ldots, w_n</span> and <span class=\"m\">x_1, \\ldots, x_n</span> are independent, the <span class=\"m\">w_i</span> have mean 0 and variance <span class=\"m\">\\sigma_w^2</span>, and the <span class=\"m\">x_i</span> have mean 0 and variance <span class=\"m\">\\sigma_x^2</span>, then <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{Var}\\left(\\sum_iw_ix_i\\right) = n\\sigma_w^2\\sigma_x^2</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-title\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Each term <span class=\"m\">w_ix_i</span> has mean <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb E[w_i]\\mathbb E[x_i] = 0</span> and variance <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb E[w_i^2x_i^2] = \\mathbb E[w_i^2]\\mathbb E[x_i^2] = \\sigma_w^2\\sigma_x^2</span>. The terms are independent, so their variances add, giving <span class=\"m\">n\\sigma_w^2\\sigma_x^2</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Check the initial loss of a 10-class classifier</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>With near-uniform predictions each class gets probability <span class=\"m\">\\frac1{10}</span>.</li>\n<li>Cross-entropy: <span class=\"m\">-\\ln\\frac1{10} = \\ln10\\approx2.303</span>.</li>\n<li>An initial loss far above this means the model is confidently wrong from the start, usually a bug or a bad initialization scale.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Clip the gradient <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf g = (6, 8)</span> at threshold <span class=\"m\">c = 5</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Norm: <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{36 + 64} = 10 &gt; 5</span>.</li>\n<li>Scale factor: <span class=\"m\">5/10 = 0.5</span>.</li>\n<li>Clipped gradient: <span class=\"m\">(3, 4)</span>, with norm 5 and the same direction.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>You train a 100-class image classifier with ReLU layers. (a) The initial loss is 4.6. Is that reasonable? (b) A hidden layer has 200 inputs. What standard deviation does He initialization use? (c) After 2 epochs the loss becomes NaN. Give two likely causes and fixes. (d) Later, training loss is 0.1 while validation loss has risen from 1.2 to 1.8 over ten epochs. Diagnose and suggest two remedies.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) Yes: <span class=\"m\">\\ln100\\approx4.605</span>, as expected for near-uniform initial predictions.\n(b) <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{2/200} = 0.1</span>.\n(c) A learning rate above the stability limit (lower it, add warmup) or an occasional huge gradient (add gradient clipping); also check for <span class=\"m\">\\log0</span> by computing the loss from logits.\n(d) Overfitting. Use early stopping at the best validation checkpoint, and add regularization such as weight decay, dropout or data augmentation.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Large training runs log the loss, gradient norm and learning rate at every step. Spikes in the gradient norm often precede loss spikes, which is why clipping is standard for transformers. Learning-rate warmup, AdamW, gradient clipping at norm 1 and cosine decay together form the default recipe for many modern models.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Reproducibility is a software engineering problem: fix random seeds, record library versions and configurations, and save checkpoints atomically. Debugging by bisection, changing one thing at a time and halving the search space, applies to training runs just as it does to code.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Pilots use checklists before every flight because experience shows that most failures come from a few simple, checkable causes. Training checklists play the same role: initial loss, one-batch overfit, learning-rate sweep and validation monitoring catch most failed runs before they waste days of compute.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Your loss becomes NaN early in training. What is the first thing to try?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">Lower the learning rate</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Add more layers</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Train longer</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Divergence is most often caused by a learning rate that is too large.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What initial cross-entropy do you expect for a 100-class classifier with near-uniform predictions? (2 decimal places)</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"4.61|4.605|4.6\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\ln100\\approx4.605</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A ReLU layer has 200 inputs. What standard deviation does He initialization use?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0.1|1/10\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{2/200} = \\sqrt{0.01}</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Clip <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf g = (6, 8)</span> at norm <span class=\"m\">c = 5</span>. What is the first entry of the clipped gradient?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The norm 10 is halved, giving <span class=\"m\">(3, 4)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A feature has mean 10 and standard deviation 2. What is the standardized value of 14?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">(14 - 10)/2 = 2</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Training loss keeps falling while validation loss rises. This indicates...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">overfitting</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">a learning rate that is too small</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">a bug in the loss function</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The model is fitting noise in the training set; use early stopping or regularization.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>"}, "formulas": [{"l": "problems", "t": "Optimization problems", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\min_\\theta f(\\theta)\\ \\text{s.t.}\\ g_i(\\theta)\\le0,\\ h_j(\\theta) = 0\\qquad\\max f = -\\min(-f)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{first order: }\\nabla f(\\theta^*) = \\mathbf 0\\qquad\\text{second order: }\\nabla^2f(\\theta^*)\\succ0\\Rightarrow\\text{strict local min}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{empirical risk: }\\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n\\ell(f_\\theta(x_i), y_i) + \\lambda R(\\theta)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\arg\\min_c\\sum_i(x_i - c)^2 = \\bar x\\qquad\\arg\\min_c\\sum_i|x_i - c| = \\operatorname{median}</span></p>"}, {"l": "convexity", "t": "Convexity", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">f(\\lambda x + (1 - \\lambda)y)\\le\\lambda f(x) + (1 - \\lambda)f(y),\\quad\\lambda\\in[0, 1]</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">f(y)\\ge f(x) + \\nabla f(x)^T(y - x)\\qquad f''\\ge0\\qquad\\nabla^2f\\succeq0</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">f(\\mathbb E[X])\\le\\mathbb E[f(X)]\\qquad(f\\text{ convex})</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{convex: }x^2,\\ e^x,\\ -\\log x,\\ |x|,\\ \\|\\mathbf x\\|,\\ \\log\\textstyle\\sum_ie^{x_i},\\ \\max(0, 1 - t)</span></p>"}, {"l": "gradient-descent", "t": "Gradient descent", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\theta_{t+1} = \\theta_t - \\eta\\nabla f(\\theta_t)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">f = \\tfrac a2x^2:\\ x_{t+1} = (1 - \\eta a)x_t,\\quad\\text{converges iff }0 &lt; \\eta &lt; \\tfrac2a,\\ \\text{best }\\eta = \\tfrac1a</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{quadratic in }n\\text{-D: }\\eta &lt; \\frac2{\\lambda_{\\max}(H)},\\qquad\\kappa = \\frac{\\lambda_{\\max}}{\\lambda_{\\min}}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">L\\text{-smooth, }\\eta = \\tfrac1L:\\quad f(\\theta_{t+1})\\le f(\\theta_t) - \\tfrac1{2L}\\|\\nabla f(\\theta_t)\\|^2</span></p>"}, {"l": "sgd", "t": "Stochastic and mini-batch gradient descent", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">f(\\theta) = \\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n\\ell_i(\\theta),\\qquad\\mathbf g_B = \\frac1{|B|}\\sum_{i\\in B}\\nabla\\ell_i(\\theta),\\qquad\\mathbb E[\\mathbf g_B] = \\nabla f(\\theta)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\operatorname{sd}(\\mathbf g_B) = \\frac\\sigma{\\sqrt B}\\qquad\\text{steps per epoch} = \\frac nB</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\sum_t\\eta_t = \\infty,\\quad\\sum_t\\eta_t^2 &lt; \\infty\\qquad\\text{(e.g. }\\eta_t = \\eta_0/t\\text{)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\eta_t = \\eta_{\\min} + \\tfrac12(\\eta_{\\max} - \\eta_{\\min})\\left(1 + \\cos\\tfrac{\\pi t}{T}\\right)</span></p>"}, {"l": "adam", "t": "Momentum, RMSProp, Adam and AdamW", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{Momentum: }\\mathbf v\\leftarrow\\beta\\mathbf v + \\mathbf g,\\quad\\theta\\leftarrow\\theta - \\eta\\mathbf v</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{RMSProp: }\\mathbf s\\leftarrow\\rho\\mathbf s + (1 - \\rho)\\mathbf g^2,\\quad\\theta\\leftarrow\\theta - \\eta\\,\\mathbf g/(\\sqrt{\\mathbf s} + \\epsilon)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Adam: }\\mathbf m\\leftarrow\\beta_1\\mathbf m + (1 - \\beta_1)\\mathbf g,\\ \\mathbf v\\leftarrow\\beta_2\\mathbf v + (1 - \\beta_2)\\mathbf g^2,\\ \\hat{\\mathbf m} = \\tfrac{\\mathbf m}{1 - \\beta_1^t},\\ \\hat{\\mathbf v} = \\tfrac{\\mathbf v}{1 - \\beta_2^t}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\theta\\leftarrow\\theta - \\eta\\frac{\\hat{\\mathbf m}}{\\sqrt{\\hat{\\mathbf v}} + \\epsilon}\\qquad\\text{AdamW: add }-\\eta\\lambda\\theta\\text{ separately}</span></p>"}, {"l": "newton", "t": "Newton's method and second-order methods", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{roots: }x_{n+1} = x_n - \\frac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}\\qquad\\text{minimization: }x_{n+1} = x_n - \\frac{f'(x_n)}{f''(x_n)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\nabla^2f(\\theta)\\,\\mathbf d = -\\nabla f(\\theta),\\qquad\\theta\\leftarrow\\theta + \\mathbf d</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">|x_{n+1} - x^*|\\le C|x_n - x^*|^2\\qquad\\text{(quadratic convergence)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{secant: }B_{t+1}\\mathbf s_t = \\mathbf y_t,\\ \\mathbf s_t = \\theta_{t+1} - \\theta_t,\\ \\mathbf y_t = \\nabla f_{t+1} - \\nabla f_t\\qquad\\text{Gauss-Newton: }H\\approx J^TJ</span></p>"}, {"l": "kkt", "t": "Constrained optimization, Lagrange multipliers, duality and KKT", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\mathcal L(\\mathbf x, \\boldsymbol\\lambda, \\boldsymbol\\mu) = f(\\mathbf x) + \\sum_i\\lambda_ig_i(\\mathbf x) + \\sum_j\\mu_jh_j(\\mathbf x)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{KKT: }\\nabla f + \\sum_i\\lambda_i\\nabla g_i + \\sum_j\\mu_j\\nabla h_j = \\mathbf 0,\\quad g_i\\le0,\\ h_j = 0,\\quad\\lambda_i\\ge0,\\quad\\lambda_ig_i = 0</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">q(\\boldsymbol\\lambda, \\boldsymbol\\mu) = \\min_{\\mathbf x}\\mathcal L\\le f^*\\ \\ (\\boldsymbol\\lambda\\ge0)\\qquad\\text{strong duality: }\\max q = f^*</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\frac{df^*}{dc} = -\\mu\\ \\text{ for the constraint }h(\\mathbf x) - c = 0</span></p>"}, {"l": "linear-programming", "t": "Linear programming", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\max\\ \\mathbf c^T\\mathbf x\\ \\ \\text{s.t.}\\ A\\mathbf x\\le\\mathbf b,\\ \\mathbf x\\ge\\mathbf 0\\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad\\min\\ \\mathbf b^T\\mathbf y\\ \\ \\text{s.t.}\\ A^T\\mathbf y\\ge\\mathbf c,\\ \\mathbf y\\ge\\mathbf 0</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{weak duality: }\\mathbf c^T\\mathbf x\\le\\mathbf b^T\\mathbf y\\qquad\\text{strong duality: }\\max\\mathbf c^T\\mathbf x = \\min\\mathbf b^T\\mathbf y</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{complementary slackness: }y_i\\,(b_i - (A\\mathbf x)_i) = 0</span></p>"}, {"l": "practical", "t": "Practical training and debugging", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{initial cross-entropy}\\approx\\ln K\\qquad\\operatorname{Var}\\Big(\\sum_{i=1}^nw_ix_i\\Big) = n\\operatorname{Var}(w)\\operatorname{Var}(x)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{He: }\\operatorname{Var}(w) = \\frac2{n_{\\text{in}}}\\qquad\\text{Xavier: }\\operatorname{Var}(w) = \\frac2{n_{\\text{in}} + n_{\\text{out}}}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{standardize: }x\\mapsto\\frac{x - \\mu}\\sigma\\qquad\\text{clip: }\\mathbf g\\leftarrow\\mathbf g\\min\\Big(1, \\frac c{\\|\\mathbf g\\|}\\Big)</span></p>"}]});
